{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FmkLrKN65hnTncMreQ4nxV","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Director's Plan\n2\t\n3\tBuild the game from this document. Nobody can answer questions, so where the plan is silent, decide and move on. TASK.md stays the authority on what to deliver, how it is named, what to report and how you may test. This plan decides what the game is and how it is built. Paths are relative to your dev folder.\n4\t\n5\t**How to read the numbers.** ✔ means I ran it on this computer on 2026-11-04 with three@0.180.0 and @dimforge/rapier3d-compat@0.19.3 and it behaved as described. Skill results are written sharp / average / clumsy, from the calibrated bots in section 17. They are simulations of a player, not a player. No mark means a starting value: tune it with the dev sliders and the balance test.\n6\t\n7\t**Contents:** 1 The game · 2 Originality rules · 3 Controls · 4 The balance model · 5 Programme and progress · 6 Events · 7 Screens · 8 Cast and writing · 9 Art bible · 10 Animation · 11 Audio · 12 Multiplayer · 13 Attract mode · 14 Dev mode · 15 Touch-only · 16 Architecture and build · 17 Testing · 18 Build order and cut order · 19 Done and reporting · 20 What was and wasn't verified · 21 TASK.md coverage and proof · 22 Planning-stage report\n8\t\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\t## 1. The game\n12\t\n13\t**Pitch.** A wooden-toy wellness retreat stands on an island balanced on a single rock, and the island has been crooked for as long as anyone remembers. You arrive as a new guest, a jointed peg doll. Every morning a fussy talking spirit level named Trudy checks you in, asks something too personal and tells you your Wobble Age. Then you go to class: hold poses with a heron, do reps under a log for a walrus, step to city pop on the bandstand, catch fruit in a basket hat, stack stones on a slab over your head. Every class pours marbles into the counterweight jar, and the whole island visibly straightens, day by day, until the morning Trudy's bubble sits in the middle for the first time.\n14\t\n15\t**Title.** Do TASK.md's title step first, since it names your folder and file. The title is a joke on the source's name, never the name itself. Keep it in one constant (`src/data/config.js`). Nothing in the fiction depends on it: the resort is the Level Best Retreat, and the cast and events have their own names.\n16\t\n17\t**Scope.** A feature-complete demo: a complete, polished vertical slice with every system in place, long enough to show everything the game can do. A full pass of the programme runs about 45 minutes.\n18\t\n19\t**Genre:** physics-balance party sports with a daily-ritual progression. **Perspective:** third person. Each event has its own framed stage camera. On the island it is a chase camera you can orbit.\n20\t\n21\t| What makes the source itself | Our version |\n22\t|---|---|\n23\t| You see your own balance as a dot in a target | The Balance Disc, in every event, driven by real physics |\n24\t| A rectangle given a personality, who asks awkward questions and notices when you skip days | Trudy, a talking spirit level whose bubble is her eye |\n25\t| Calm, slightly uncanny instructors; \"the trainer notices when you slack off\" | Ms. Stillwater (a heron) and Coach Bolster (a walrus), who both notice |\n26\t| A daily test that hands you an embarrassing age | The Check-In and your Wobble Age |\n27\t| Four kinds of training | Stillness, Strength, Cardio, Balance Play |\n28\t| Minutes trained fill a bank that opens new things | Marbles pour into a glass jar, physically |\n29\t| The family takes turns and laughs at each other | Group Class on one TV; an AI Hub class across devices |\n30\t| Absurd little games between the earnest ones | Fruit Hat, Springboard, Tippy Raft |\n31\t\n32\t**Pillars. When in doubt, serve these.**\n33\t1. **One verb: shift your weight.** Every event reads the same Balance Dot from the same physics. Learn it once and you can play everything.\n34\t2. **Physics tells the jokes.** Falling is the best moment in the game: clattering wooden limbs, a straight-faced line from Trudy, a spring back up. Failure is never punishing.\n35\t3. **A gentle ritual.** Check in, train, watch the marbles, go to bed. No skill gate ever stops the story.\n36\t4. **The world is the progress bar.** The lighthouse leans 9° on Day 1 and stands straight on the last day. Loose props slide to the low side. The music is slightly out of tune until the island is level.\n37\t5. **The room laughs.** It is played on a TV at 10 feet, and it should be funny to watch.\n38\t\n39\t**The first five minutes** (build this exact beat, it is the game's first impression):\n40\t1. Title screen at dawn. The crooked island fills the frame. The title's letters stand on a wooden beam that tilts with the island, and they slide a little. After 10 idle seconds, attract mode starts.\n41\t2. Press to start. \"Who's here today?\" Four empty mats. Pick one. The name spinner offers a generated name (\"Gentle Margaret\"); reroll or accept. Pick a paint colour. Under a minute.\n42\t3. You drop onto the island from above, clatter, spring up. Trudy hops over, bubble sliding to the high side. \"Oh! A person. Please step on. Not on me. On the island.\"\n43\t4. Trudy asks you to lean left. As you do, **the whole island tips under you** and every orange on the island rolls. No menu has taught this; the world did. Lean right, then forward. \"I can feel that, by the way.\"\n44\t5. First Check-In. Three short tests. The drum roll. \"Your Wobble Age is 74. Some of my favourite people are 74. Mostly the ones sitting down.\"\n45\t6. Walk to the Sun Deck (or pick it from Today's Plan) for your first pose with Ms. Stillwater, then Fruit Hat on the pier, where you get greedy, fall over, and laugh.\n46\t7. Marbles pour into the jar. The island creaks and comes up one notch. The lighthouse is visibly straighter. Bed.\n47\t\n48\t---\n49\t\n50\t## 2. Originality rules\n51\t\n52\tThis is an original game in the source's genre, with similar kinds of mechanics. It is not a recreation.\n53\t\n54\t- **Never show** the source's name on screen (outside the title's joke and the About screen's Source line), nor use its product or feature names (balance board, the words it uses for its test, age or bank), its characters, its avatar style, its music or its look. No white-and-pale-blue gym, no plain white-clad instructor, no Mii-style avatars (ours are jointed wooden peg dolls), no talking board, no piggy bank.\n55\t- **Never copy its activities.** No ski jump, slalom, soccer heading, table tilt with balls and holes, tightrope, hula hoop, penguin slide, river bubble, snowboard or boxing. The events in section 6 replace them with our own ideas. Add new ones in that spirit, not from that list.\n56\t- **Tone guard.** Trudy teases posture, knees, commitment and attendance. **Nothing in the game comments on body weight, body shape or food.** There is no scale, no BMI and no weight number. \"Weight\" only ever means where you are leaning. No real health claims.\n57\t- All art, music, text and voices are made for this game, in code.\n58\t\n59\t---\n60\t\n61\t## 3. Controls\n62\t\n63\tDesign for a PS5 DualSense, or any pad the browser reports with `mapping === 'standard'`, on a TV at 10 feet. Keyboard and mouse play the same game on the same screens, with no separate desktop layout, and they should feel native rather than like stand-ins. Weight comes from a stick on a controller and from the pointer with a mouse. Both are analog. **Stick and mouse are always screen-relative:** up is away from the camera, right is screen right. Rotate the input by the camera's yaw before using it.\n64\t\n65\t| Verb | Controller (standard mapping) | Keyboard | Mouse |\n66\t|---|---|---|---|\n67\t| Shift weight (events) / walk (island) | Left stick, axes 0 and 1 | W A S D or arrow keys (see below); C recentres | Pointer's offset from screen centre, normalised by 33% of screen height, clamped to the unit disc |\n68\t| Left foot | L2, button 6, analog `.value` | Q | Left button |\n69\t| Right foot | R2, button 7, analog `.value` | E | Right button |\n70\t| Hop / both feet | Cross, button 0 | Space | Both buttons |\n71\t| Flail | Square, button 2 | F | Middle button |\n72\t| Breathe in (hold) | R1, button 5 | Left Shift | |\n73\t| Ask Trudy (island) | Triangle, button 3 | T | |\n74\t| Look (island only) | Right stick, axes 2 and 3 | | Hold right button and drag; wheel zooms |\n75\t| Use / confirm | Cross | Enter or E (island), Enter or Space (menus) | Left click |\n76\t| Back | Circle, button 1 | Esc or Backspace | Right click (menus only; suppress the context menu) |\n77\t| Pause | Options, button 9 | Esc | |\n78\t| Menu move | D-pad or left stick | Arrow keys or W A S D | Hover moves the focus |\n79\t\n80\t- **No pointer lock is needed**, which keeps the game simple inside the hub's frames. Hide the cursor during events when the mouse is in use: the faint ring on the Balance Disc shows where it is asking you to go.\n81\t- **Which device moves your weight:** whichever was used last. With a pad, ignore the pointer. With keyboard and mouse, keys win while a weight key is down, otherwise the pointer, once it has moved. A resting mouse never fights a resting stick.\n82\t- **Keys in balance events ✔: they move the asking ring, and it stays where you leave it.** Holding a key moves the ring at 1.6 units/s; releasing leaves it there, and C recentres. The ring must be clearly visible on the Balance Disc, because a player steering by keys watches the ring, not the dot. I tested three models with a person-like bot. Keys that spring back on release needed 9.6 presses a second just to hold a pose. A tap-then-accelerate model scored worst (Poses Tier 1: 68 / 75 / 38). This one holds up against the stick: Poses Tier 1 98 / 62 / 54 (stick 100 / 76 / 63), Tier 2 86 / 58 / 34 (90 / 64 / 53), Still Water Tier 1 100 / 85 / 28 (100 / 93 / 24), Fruit Hat Tier 1 21 / 22 / 20 banked with no falls (28 / 33 / 28), Room Service Tier 1 all five glasses. Use a move speed of about 1.6 and don't add acceleration.\n83\t- **Keys when you're moving, not balancing: direct.** Walking on the island, Tippy Raft, steering in Island Loop and spinning in Springboard's air use the raw key direction, as PC players expect. Plate Spinners has its own scheme ✔, below.\n84\t- **Foot keys** ramp 0 to 1 in 0.12 s.\n85\t- **Latch presses ✔.** A key or mouse button pressed and released between two input polls must still count, or quick taps vanish at low frame rates. Normalise key codes too: the hub's harness sends synthetic events whose `code` is just `a`, so accept `a` and `KeyA` alike.\n86\t- **Read mouse buttons from the `buttons` bitmask ✔.** Pointer Events don't fire `pointerdown` for a second button while one is already held, so with left and right as the two feet, \"both buttons\" (Hop, Log Reps, Springboard) never registers if you only listen for `pointerdown`. Read `e.buttons` on down, up and move: bit 1 is left, 2 is right, 4 is middle.\n87\t- **Hover means the pointer moved ✔.** Move focus only on a `pointermove` whose coordinates changed. A browser also fires `pointerenter` when a menu appears under a parked mouse, and that made focus jump by itself under a controller player.\n88\t- **On the island,** a hovered thing (pavilion, jar, board, Trudy) gets a thick outline and a label. Left click walks there and uses it. WASD and E work without the mouse. Hit-test by projecting each hotspot's anchor to screen and taking the nearest within a radius. It is simple and robust.\n89\t- **Prompts follow the last input.** Track `lastDevice` (`pad` or `kbm`): a pad counts when a stick passes 0.25 or a button is pressed; keyboard and mouse count on keydown, mousedown or 4+ px of movement. Prompts re-render the same frame. A Settings option switches pad glyphs between PlayStation shapes and Xbox letters (A B X Y, LT RT LB RB). Keyboard prompts show keycaps, mouse prompts show a mouse with the button lit. Draw all glyphs as inline SVG in code. Every glyph element carries a `data-glyph` attribute that tests can read.\n90\t- **On the island,** Cross and E use the focused thing if there is one; otherwise Cross and Space hop.\n91\t- **Create + Options belongs to the hub.** Ignore Options while Create (button 8) is held, everywhere.\n92\t- **If a player's pad disconnects mid-class,** pause and say so on screen.\n93\t- **Haptics:** `pad.vibrationActuator?.playEffect('dual-rumble', …)` on falls, catches, rising fatigue and near-tip. Guard it, with a Settings toggle.\n94\t- **Audio on a controller ✔.** Call `AudioContext.resume()` on pad input too, not only on keys and clicks. My first wiring left a controller-only player in silence. I can't confirm here that every browser accepts a gamepad press as the gesture that unlocks audio, so if the context is still suspended a few seconds after pad input, show a small tag asking for a key or click.\n95\t\n96\t---\n97\t\n98\t## 4. The balance model\n99\t\n100\tThis is the heart of the game and it is the most tested part of this plan. Copy it, then tune it.\n101\t\n102\t**The doll's \"balance core\"** is one Rapier dynamic body on a ball joint at the foot. Everything in the game that stands uses it. Its colliders can carry props, and those props push back on it through real contacts, so a heavy hat makes you top-heavy without any special code.\n103\t\n104\t```js\n105\t// m = 1, h = 0.75 (foot to centre of mass), doll stands 1.5 m tall. Fixed step 1/120 s.\n106\tconst body = world.createRigidBody(RAPIER.RigidBodyDesc.dynamic()\n107\t  .setTranslation(fx, fy + 0.75, fz).setCanSleep(false).setAdditionalSolverIterations(4));\n108\tworld.createCollider(RAPIER.ColliderDesc.capsule(0.5, 0.25).setMass(1).setCollisionGroups(DOLL(lane)), body);\n109\tconst joint = world.createImpulseJoint(\n110\t  RAPIER.JointData.spherical({x:0,y:0,z:0}, {x:0,y:-0.75,z:0}), footBody, body, true);\n111\tjoint.setContactsEnabled(false);            // footBody is kinematicPositionBased\n112\t\n113\tconst G = 9.81 * 0.75, I = 0.75 * 0.75 + 0.06;   // gravity moment, inertia about the foot\n114\tconst K = G * (1 + margin), wn = Math.sqrt((K - G) / I), D = 2 * zeta * wn * I;\n115\t\n116\t// Each fixed step, before world.step():\n117\tconst r = 1 - Math.exp(-reflex * dt);\n118\twx += (wishX - wx) * r;  wz += (wishZ - wz) * r;       // wish = the input, on the unit disc\n119\tconst tx = wx * maxLean, tz = wz * maxLean;            // target lean in radians\n120\tconst q = body.rotation();\n121\tconst ux = 2*(q.x*q.y - q.w*q.z), uy = 1 - 2*(q.x*q.x + q.z*q.z), uz = 2*(q.y*q.z + q.w*q.x);\n122\tleanX = Math.atan2(ux, uy);  leanZ = Math.atan2(uz, uy);   // lean toward +x and +z\n123\tconst w = body.angvel();\n124\tlet Tx = -(G*Math.sin(tx) + K*(leanX - tx)) + D*w.z;    // gravity feed-forward, spring, damper\n125\tlet Tz = -(G*Math.sin(tz) + K*(leanZ - tz)) - D*w.x;\n126\tconst limit = ankleMax * (flailLeft > 0 ? flailBoost : 1);\n127\tconst m = Math.hypot(Tx, Tz);  if (m > limit) { Tx *= limit/m; Tz *= limit/m; }\n128\t// nx, nz are the disturbances (sway, tremor, gusts, shoves), in the same lean space, not clamped\n129\tbody.resetTorques(true);\n130\tbody.addTorque({ x: Tz + nz, y: -w.y * 0.6, z: -(Tx + nx) }, true);\n131\t```\n132\t\n133\t**Defaults ✔:** `maxLean` 18°, `margin` 0.45, `zeta` 0.40, `ankleMax` 4.6 N·m, `reflex` 10 /s, `flailBoost` 1.9, flail lasts 0.35 s with a 2 s cooldown, fall at 45° of lean.\n134\t\n135\t**What those numbers do ✔:**\n136\t- Full stick from rest reaches 90% in about 1 s, overshoots to about 23° and settles at 18°. It is a soft, wobbly, toy-like feel.\n137\t- Whipping the stick end to end at about 0.5 Hz survives. Slamming it from −18° to +18° peaks near 28° at `ankleMax` 4.2 and up, and falls at 3.8. This is why careful correction matters.\n138\t- The torque limit makes a natural point of no return at `asin(ankleMax / G)`, about 39° with no load. Hat and stone loads lower it automatically.\n139\t\n140\t**Gusts at the defaults ✔ (0.5 s long; these numbers are the difficulty dial):**\n141\t\n142\t| Gust | Do nothing | Flail as you pass about 30° | Stick against it once it has started | Braced: stick at 60% against it, 0.5 s before it hits |\n143\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n144\t| 2 N·m | safe, peaks 22° | | | |\n145\t| 2.5 N·m | safe, peaks 31° | | | |\n146\t| 3 N·m | **falls** | saved, peaks 38° | falls | safe, peaks 19° |\n147\t| 3.5 N·m | falls | saved only if timed near 32°, peaks 43° | falls | safe, peaks 29° |\n148\t| 4 N·m | falls | falls | falls | falls |\n149\t\n150\tSo a telegraph is worth a lot: **give a warning before every gust of 3 N·m or more.** And flail is a real last-moment save with timing in it: a flail used early, before you have moved much, wears off and you still fall. It is the moment people will shout at the TV.\n151\t\n152\t**The load rule ✔.** Every prop adds a gravity moment δ = mass × 9.81 × height. The doll grows unstable when total δ exceeds K − G (3.3 at the default margin). A prop's mass therefore decides how many you can carry. Too heavy and the doll tips over at once: with margin 0.45, 0.06 kg fruit tipped it at the 5th or 6th catch, while 0.015 kg works out to about 13. **Keep prop masses small** (0.01 to 0.03 kg) against the doll's 1 kg.\n153\t\n154\t**Slow sway is what makes holding still a game ✔.** A thumb is far steadier than a body on a board, so a player who simply parks the stick on a target and leaves it scored 100% in my first test. Fast tremor barely moves the dot. The fix is slow forcing that the player has to see and counter by hand. Add it to `nx`, `nz`:\n155\t\n156\t```js\n157\tnx = sway  *(Math.sin(t*0.9 + ph)*0.6 + Math.sin(t*1.73 + ph*2.1)*0.4)\n158\t   + tremor*(Math.sin(t*8.2 + 1.1)*0.6 + Math.sin(t*18.3)*0.4);\n159\tnz = sway  *(Math.sin(t*0.77 + ph*1.7)*0.6 + Math.sin(t*1.51 + ph*0.6)*0.4)\n160\t   + tremor*(Math.sin(t*7.1 + 2.3)*0.6 + Math.sin(t*15.7 + 0.7)*0.4);   // ph from the event's seed\n161\t```\n162\tSway units are N·m. Sway of 0.2 moves the dot about a quarter of the disc if ignored. Tremor is mostly for looks and rumble.\n163\t\n164\t**Traps I hit ✔:**\n165\t- **The doll's colliders must not touch the ground.** The capsule's foot rotating into the floor silently limits lean to about 8° and every event feels dead. Use collision groups: `(membership << 16) | filter`. Bits: `GROUND 0x0001`, props of lane *i* `0x0010 << i`, doll of lane *i* `0x0100 << i`, ragdoll parts `0x1000`. A doll meets only its own lane's props. A prop meets its own lane's doll, its own lane's props and the ground. Ragdoll parts meet only the ground. Lanes never touch each other.\n166\t- **Stacked things use cuboid colliders.** Convex-hull stones collapsed by themselves with no disturbance at all (5th stone, every seed). Plain cuboids held. Draw a rounded stone over a box collider.\n167\t- **Foot motion must be smooth.** With a kinematic foot bobbing 3 cm, or moving in stop-start strides, everything left the tray. Steps set a target speed and the foot low-passes toward it. Bob the visual only.\n168\t- Use `world.integrationParameters.numSolverIterations = 8` to `10` for events with props.\n169\t- **A Rapier panic kills the physics for the whole page ✔.** It surfaces as `RuntimeError: unreachable` from the WASM module, and no later call recovers. I hit two causes, both of them easy to write by accident:\n170\t  1. **Touching a body after removing it, or removing it twice.** Drop every reference when you remove a body: remove it from your lists in the same line.\n171\t  2. **Removing a prop and the collider it rested on in the same step, or the support first and the prop later.** `world.step()` panics about half a second later, once the body moves. Even one stone does it. It looks fine if nothing moves, so a quick test can miss it. **Safe: remove the prop, step once, then remove the support.** `collider.setEnabled(false)` and `body.setEnabled(false)` are safe too, and so is freeing the whole world.\n172\t  Everything else the plan does soaked clean ✔: ragdoll spawn and remove 30 times with the doll swaying, four ragdolls at once, fruit loaded and removed 40 times, removing and re-creating the foot joint 30 times, plate shards 40 times, tumblers respawned 30 times, 60 fresh worlds in a row, props removed on a gravity-tilted island. If a panic still happens: out of a session, save and reload the page; in a session, call `AIHub.fail(message)`. Never catch it and carry on.\n173\t\n174\t**The Balance Disc.** `cob = (leanX, leanZ) / maxLean` is the dot, shown truthfully. Draw it as inline SVG and move it with transforms each frame. It is the game's signature HUD: a large circular dial with rings, a target zone that turns green when you are inside, the solid dot, a faint ring showing where your stick is asking to go, and a short inked trail of the last half second, like a seismograph needle. Diameter is about 22% of screen height, bottom left. Trudy peeks over its rim and reacts. In Group Class each player gets a smaller one (14%) along the bottom edge.\n175\t\n176\t**Falling.** At 45° of lean the doll becomes a ragdoll (section 10) with a clatter of wooden clacks, a quip, a rumble and a comic \"CLACK!\" word in the world. After `recoverTime` (2.2 s) the parts snap back with a spring noise and the doll bounces upright. Only the current hold or rep is lost. Disable the core while the ragdoll runs (`body.setEnabled(false)`). On recovery reset its pose, velocities and **your cached `leanX`, `leanZ`** ✔: if you forget, the stale lean makes the doll fall again at once, for ever. Falls are counted in the Journal as Floor Exercises.\n177\t\n178\t**Flail.** One button, all devices. Arms windmill for 0.35 s and the ankle gets `flailBoost` strength. Cooldown is 2 s. It looks ridiculous on purpose. It is the save that makes near-falls exciting, and it only works if you time it (see the table above). A flail costs a small form penalty in Stillness events, and the instructor comments.\n179\t\n180\t**Breath.** In Stillness events a breath ring pulses: 4 s growing, 4 s shrinking. Hold Breathe while it grows, release while it shrinks. Over the last full cycle, 70%+ in sync multiplies sway and tremor by 0.6 and form gain by 1.25. Out of sync does nothing bad except miss the bonus. Accessibility toggle: auto-breathe. It is worth a lot ✔: perfectly in sync, Poses Tier 1 goes 95→100 / 66→91 / 54→78, and The Cairn Tier 2 goes from 5.3 / 4.8 / 2 stones of 8 to 8 / 8 / 6.3.\n181\t\n182\t**Difficulty** (Settings): Gentle, Standard (default), Honest. It multiplies `ankleMax` by 1.25 / 1 / 0.9, disturbances by 0.65 / 1 / 1.2 and rhythm windows by 1.4 / 1 / 0.8.\n183\t\n184\t---\n185\t\n186\t## 5. Programme and progress\n187\t\n188\t**Days are game days, not calendar days.** A day ends when you go to bed. The real date is only used for absence jokes and calendar stamps.\n189\t\n190\t| Day | Name | Check-In tests | Classes | What it teaches |\n191\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n192\t| 1 | Arrival | Stand Still, Lean Match, Step Time | Poses, Fruit Hat | Lean, flail, breath, the jar |\n193\t| 2 | The Second Day, Which Is Harder | Stand Still, Lean Match, Quick Lean | Log Reps, Step Class, The Cairn | Feet, rhythm, stacking |\n194\t| 3 | Service With a Wobble | One-Foot Hold, Lean Match, Step Time | Room Service, Plate Spinners, Plank & Perch | Stepping, stick circles. The Mirror opens |\n195\t| 4 | A Little Damp | Quick Lean, One-Foot Hold, Stand Still | Springboard, Still Water, Island Loop | Air control. Group Class invitation |\n196\t| 5 | Level Day | Your three best tests | Tippy Raft, Tug of Peace, The Grand Hold | The ending |\n197\t| 6+ | Maintenance | Random three | Trudy's random plan of three | Free Play continues forever |\n198\t\n199\tBuild the day list from the event registry: an event that was cut (section 18) is skipped and everything still works.\n200\t\n201\t**A day's flow:** morning line → personal question (Yes/No) → Check-In → Wobble Age reveal → Today's Plan (cards in any order: walk there or pick a card to go directly) → each class → Results → marbles → the island comes up a notch → extra Free Play if wanted → the bed hut. Press Use, the screen inks out, a stamp lands on the calendar, the next dawn. Palettes follow the day's steps (dawn, noon, golden, dusk), unless an event pins its own (Step Class at dusk, The Grand Hold at sunset).\n202\t\n203\t**The Check-In** (about 90 s) takes place on a round mat with Trudy. Three tests, each one screen with one big instruction. Each scores 0 to 1 (clamped):\n204\t- **Stand Still** (10 s, sway .1, tremor .06): keep the dot in the centre. Score `1 − meanDistance / .25` ✔ .82 / .74 / .49.\n205\t- **Lean Match** (18 s, sway .04): \"Lean 70% left.\" A bar shows your percentage. Three targets, 6 s each, ignoring the first 1.5 s of each. Score `1 − meanError / .4` ✔ .58 / .45 / .27. (My first version, hold within ±5% for 2 s, was impossible: every bot timed out.)\n206\t- **Step Time** (16 s): alternate feet with a metronome at 96 BPM. Score `1 − meanError / 120 ms` ✔ .81 / .63 / .37.\n207\t- **Quick Lean** (15 s): lean toward the lit quadrant, six prompts. Score `1 − (meanReaction − .35 s) / .9 s` ✔ .87 / .69 / .44.\n208\t- **One-Foot Hold** (12 s): keep R2 down and L2 up, which halves `margin`, while keeping the dot centred. Score as Stand Still ✔ .55 / .48 / .40.\n209\t\n210\t**Wobble Age.** `age = round(18 + 81 × (1 − mean)^1.3 + handicap)`, clamped to 18..99. The handicap is a comic head start that shrinks each day: +24, +16, +8, +3, then 0. So the number always falls, and that is what makes coming back feel good. With unchanged skill, Day 1 to Day 5 ✔: 56→32 / 66→42 / 86→62. The reveal is the comic set piece: lights dim, drum roll, numbers spin on a wooden counter, sting, Trudy's line from a band of lines by age. Stored per day and drawn as the Journal's graph. It never asks your real age (\"I didn't ask how old you are. I asked your knees.\").\n211\t\n212\t**Marbles.** After each class: 2 per minute of active play (min 2) + medal bonus (1, 2, 3, 5) + 2 for a first clear. Each Check-In gives 1. They pour physically into the jar on the island, as real Rapier balls: a 200-marble pour runs fine (✔ 0.23 ms per step; 500 is 0.67 ms). Marble colour shows the medal. Thresholds unlock hats and paints (`src/data/unlocks.js`: 6, 14, 24, 36, 50, 60 marbles). A full programme pays about 15, 37, 59, 81, 103 by the end of each day, or 61 in total with all Tier B events cut, so everything unlocks either way ✔.\n213\t\n214\t**The island's tilt depends on progress, not skill.** Rest tilt = 9° × (1 − completed programme steps / total steps), so nobody can be stuck. Each step lowers it with a long wooden creak, all loose props shuffling and Trudy reacting. The last notch happens inside The Grand Hold.\n215\t\n216\t**Medals** come from form (0 to 100): ≥40 Adequate, ≥65 Pleasant, ≥85 Exemplary, ≥97 Suspicious. Below 40 is still a pass (\"Attended\"). Section 6 gives an event's form where it has its own formula. Everywhere else, form = 100 × raw / `par`, with `par` set per tier so the sharp bot lands 88 to 95 (section 17).\n217\t\n218\t**Tiers.** Every event has three: Gentle, Honest, Unreasonable. Tier 2 opens with a Pleasant on Tier 1, Tier 3 with an Exemplary on Tier 2. Days 1 to 5 use Tier 1. Maintenance days use the highest tier opened. Free Play offers all open tiers.\n219\t\n220\t**Free Play** opens each event once it has been met in the programme. Walk to its pavilion, or use the menu.\n221\t\n222\t**The ending.** The Grand Hold (section 6) ends with the island level. Trudy's bubble centres for the first time. She has only ever seen the world at nine degrees. \"Oh. So that's what the middle looks like.\" Silence, then the marbles flare, the lighthouse lights, and the credits roll over a slow pan of everything you straightened. Then: \"It's quite flat, isn't it? ...Same time tomorrow?\" Save the \"levelled\" state, unlock Free Play for everything, and carry on into Maintenance days.\n223\t\n224\t**Profiles.** Up to four, each with a name, paint, hat, days, ages, medals, marbles and counts. Name is a spinner over generated names (no text entry; `src/data/names.js`). Save JSON in `localStorage` with a version number, read inside try/catch, and fall back to memory if storage is unavailable.\n225\t\n226\t---\n227\t\n228\t## 6. Events\n229\t\n230\t### Common rules\n231\t\n232\t**What you can and can't do.** Every Intro Card and the How to Play page say what you can do here and, in one line, what you can't: \"You can't fall in this one.\" \"Your feet don't matter here.\" \"You can't lose this; you can only do it badly.\" A player should never have to guess whether a thing is possible.\n233\t\n234\t**Flow.** Intro Card (name, one sentence, what you can and can't do, one diagram, this device's prompts; Use to begin) → instructor counts 3-2-1 → play → whistle or bell → Results (ink-stamp medal, one big number, one line of stats, a line of dialogue, retry or continue) → marbles in the programme.\n235\t\n236\t**Lanes.** Every event is written for an array of lanes from the first line: `for (const lane of this.lanes)`. A lane is `{ id, kind: 'local' | 'bot' | 'ghost', input, doll, balancer, props, score, name, colour }`. Solo is one lane. Group Class is up to four local lanes. A hub session is one local lane plus ghost lanes. Attract mode uses bot lanes. Lanes sit 3.2 m apart on x. Each has its own collision bits. Ghost lanes have no physics at all (section 12). The camera frames all lanes in Group Class, and your own lane with neighbours partly visible in a session. Field of view 38° ✔ (the look tests used it). Pull back to fit lanes: `d = max(base, (spread / 2 + 2.5) / tan(hfov / 2))`. Chase camera: about (0, 3.4, 9) behind the doll, damped at 6 /s.\n237\t\n238\t**Each event provides:** `buildSet()`, `start()`, `fixed(dt)`, `frame(dt)`, `laneResult(i)`, `botInput(i, t, skill)`, `dispose()`, plus static `id, title, discipline, instructor, tiers, couch, session, sessionConfig`. A fresh Rapier world per event, freed in `dispose()`: no state leaks. Gameplay randomness comes only from `rng('event-id')` (section 16).\n239\t\n240\t**Non-standing poses reuse the balance core:** in Plank & Perch, Still Water and Tug of Peace the core stays upright and invisible, and the visible doll plays the pose and rocks with it. Nothing in the rules knows the difference.\n241\t\n242\t### STILLNESS, with Ms. Stillwater\n243\t\n244\t**Poses** (Sun Deck, a round teak deck on stilts over the water; low three-quarter camera, heron beside you)\n245\t- *Play:* The heron demonstrates, then the target ring glides from centre to the pose's spot on the disc (3 s). Follow it, then hold while the ring shrinks. Breath ring runs throughout.\n246\t- *Tiers ✔:* maxLean 14°, margin 0.45. Tier 1: 3 poses, hold 8 s, ring 0.30→0.16, sway 0.08→0.22, tremor 0.08→0.20. Tier 2: 4 poses, hold 10 s, ring 0.30→0.13, sway 0.12→0.30, tremor 0.10→0.30. Tier 3: 5 poses, hold 12 s, ring 0.30→0.10, sway 0.15→0.38, tremor 0.15→0.40.\n247\t- *Scoring ✔:* each tick in the ring earns `0.30 / ringRadius`; form is the share of the maximum, capped at 100. Without breath: Tier 1 95 / 66 / 54, Tier 2 74 / 58 / 46, Tier 3 55 / 41 / 33. In sync throughout: Tier 1 100 / 91 / 78, Tier 2 100 / 85 / 71, Tier 3 96 / 62 / 57. Tiers 2 and 3 are hard on purpose, and breath is how you get there.\n248\t- *Pose table* (`src/data/poses.js`, spot on the disc): The Contented Heron (−.35, 0), The Apologetic Pine (0, .4), Half-Folded Deck Chair (0, −.35), The Lighthouse Keeper (.4, 0), Startled Flamingo (.35, .2), The Long Goodbye (−.2, .45), Someone Else's Umbrella (.45, −.1), The Quiet Regret (−.4, −.2). All standing poses.\n249\t- *Bot:* follow the ring with a delayed correction; breathe in sync.\n250\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cc2a51a5-f08a-42e7-afc4-8d441845bc15","uuid":"b12b9b66-ab82-4a86-a218-119bcc02e1a6","timestamp":"2026-11-05T12:22:18.960Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-052201__x29b4d9/work/PLAN.md","content":"# Director's Plan\n\nBuild the game from this document. Nobody can answer questions, so where the plan is silent, decide and move on. TASK.md stays the authority on what to deliver, how it is named, what to report and how you may test. This plan decides what the game is and how it is built. Paths are relative to your dev folder.\n\n**How to read the numbers.** ✔ means I ran it on this computer on 2026-11-04 with three@0.180.0 and @dimforge/rapier3d-compat@0.19.3 and it behaved as described. Skill results are written sharp / average / clumsy, from the calibrated bots in section 17. They are simulations of a player, not a player. No mark means a starting value: tune it with the dev sliders and the balance test.\n\n**Contents:** 1 The game · 2 Originality rules · 3 Controls · 4 The balance model · 5 Programme and progress · 6 Events · 7 Screens · 8 Cast and writing · 9 Art bible · 10 Animation · 11 Audio · 12 Multiplayer · 13 Attract mode · 14 Dev mode · 15 Touch-only · 16 Architecture and build · 17 Testing · 18 Build order and cut order · 19 Done and reporting · 20 What was and wasn't verified · 21 TASK.md coverage and proof · 22 Planning-stage report\n\n---\n\n## 1. The game\n\n**Pitch.** A wooden-toy wellness retreat stands on an island balanced on a single rock, and the island has been crooked for as long as anyone remembers. You arrive as a new guest, a jointed peg doll. Every morning a fussy talking spirit level named Trudy checks you in, asks something too personal and tells you your Wobble Age. Then you go to class: hold poses with a heron, do reps under a log for a walrus, step to city pop on the bandstand, catch fruit in a basket hat, stack stones on a slab over your head. Every class pours marbles into the counterweight jar, and the whole island visibly straightens, day by day, until the morning Trudy's bubble sits in the middle for the first time.\n\n**Title.** Do TASK.md's title step first, since it names your folder and file. The title is a joke on the source's name, never the name itself. Keep it in one constant (`src/data/config.js`). Nothing in the fiction depends on it: the resort is the Level Best Retreat, and the cast and events have their own names.\n\n**Scope.** A feature-complete demo: a complete, polished vertical slice with every system in place, long enough to show everything the game can do. A full pass of the programme runs about 45 minutes.\n\n**Genre:** physics-balance party sports with a daily-ritual progression. **Perspective:** third person. Each event has its own framed stage camera. On the island it is a chase camera you can orbit.\n\n| What makes the source itself | Our version |\n|---|---|\n| You see your own balance as a dot in a target | The Balance Disc, in every event, driven by real physics |\n| A rectangle given a personality, who asks awkward questions and notices when you skip days | Trudy, a talking spirit level whose bubble is her eye |\n| Calm, slightly uncanny instructors; \"the trainer notices when you slack off\" | Ms. Stillwater (a heron) and Coach Bolster (a walrus), who both notice |\n| A daily test that hands you an embarrassing age | The Check-In and your Wobble Age |\n| Four kinds of training | Stillness, Strength, Cardio, Balance Play |\n| Minutes trained fill a bank that opens new things | Marbles pour into a glass jar, physically |\n| The family takes turns and laughs at each other | Group Class on one TV; an AI Hub class across devices |\n| Absurd little games between the earnest ones | Fruit Hat, Springboard, Tippy Raft |\n\n**Pillars. When in doubt, serve these.**\n1. **One verb: shift your weight.** Every event reads the same Balance Dot from the same physics. Learn it once and you can play everything.\n2. **Physics tells the jokes.** Falling is the best moment in the game: clattering wooden limbs, a straight-faced line from Trudy, a spring back up. Failure is never punishing.\n3. **A gentle ritual.** Check in, train, watch the marbles, go to bed. No skill gate ever stops the story.\n4. **The world is the progress bar.** The lighthouse leans 9° on Day 1 and stands straight on the last day. Loose props slide to the low side. The music is slightly out of tune until the island is level.\n5. **The room laughs.** It is played on a TV at 10 feet, and it should be funny to watch.\n\n**The first five minutes** (build this exact beat, it is the game's first impression):\n1. Title screen at dawn. The crooked island fills the frame. The title's letters stand on a wooden beam that tilts with the island, and they slide a little. After 10 idle seconds, attract mode starts.\n2. Press to start. \"Who's here today?\" Four empty mats. Pick one. The name spinner offers a generated name (\"Gentle Margaret\"); reroll or accept. Pick a paint colour. Under a minute.\n3. You drop onto the island from above, clatter, spring up. Trudy hops over, bubble sliding to the high side. \"Oh! A person. Please step on. Not on me. On the island.\"\n4. Trudy asks you to lean left. As you do, **the whole island tips under you** and every orange on the island rolls. No menu has taught this; the world did. Lean right, then forward. \"I can feel that, by the way.\"\n5. First Check-In. Three short tests. The drum roll. \"Your Wobble Age is 74. Some of my favourite people are 74. Mostly the ones sitting down.\"\n6. Walk to the Sun Deck (or pick it from Today's Plan) for your first pose with Ms. Stillwater, then Fruit Hat on the pier, where you get greedy, fall over, and laugh.\n7. Marbles pour into the jar. The island creaks and comes up one notch. The lighthouse is visibly straighter. Bed.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Originality rules\n\nThis is an original game in the source's genre, with similar kinds of mechanics. It is not a recreation.\n\n- **Never show** the source's name on screen (outside the title's joke and the About screen's Source line), nor use its product or feature names (balance board, the words it uses for its test, age or bank), its characters, its avatar style, its music or its look. No white-and-pale-blue gym, no plain white-clad instructor, no Mii-style avatars (ours are jointed wooden peg dolls), no talking board, no piggy bank.\n- **Never copy its activities.** No ski jump, slalom, soccer heading, table tilt with balls and holes, tightrope, hula hoop, penguin slide, river bubble, snowboard or boxing. The events in section 6 replace them with our own ideas. Add new ones in that spirit, not from that list.\n- **Tone guard.** Trudy teases posture, knees, commitment and attendance. **Nothing in the game comments on body weight, body shape or food.** There is no scale, no BMI and no weight number. \"Weight\" only ever means where you are leaning. No real health claims.\n- All art, music, text and voices are made for this game, in code.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Controls\n\nDesign for a PS5 DualSense, or any pad the browser reports with `mapping === 'standard'`, on a TV at 10 feet. Keyboard and mouse play the same game on the same screens, with no separate desktop layout, and they should feel native rather than like stand-ins. Weight comes from a stick on a controller and from the pointer with a mouse. Both are analog. **Stick and mouse are always screen-relative:** up is away from the camera, right is screen right. Rotate the input by the camera's yaw before using it.\n\n| Verb | Controller (standard mapping) | Keyboard | Mouse |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Shift weight (events) / walk (island) | Left stick, axes 0 and 1 | W A S D or arrow keys (see below); C recentres | Pointer's offset from screen centre, normalised by 33% of screen height, clamped to the unit disc |\n| Left foot | L2, button 6, analog `.value` | Q | Left button |\n| Right foot | R2, button 7, analog `.value` | E | Right button |\n| Hop / both feet | Cross, button 0 | Space | Both buttons |\n| Flail | Square, button 2 | F | Middle button |\n| Breathe in (hold) | R1, button 5 | Left Shift | |\n| Ask Trudy (island) | Triangle, button 3 | T | |\n| Look (island only) | Right stick, axes 2 and 3 | | Hold right button and drag; wheel zooms |\n| Use / confirm | Cross | Enter or E (island), Enter or Space (menus) | Left click |\n| Back | Circle, button 1 | Esc or Backspace | Right click (menus only; suppress the context menu) |\n| Pause | Options, button 9 | Esc | |\n| Menu move | D-pad or left stick | Arrow keys or W A S D | Hover moves the focus |\n\n- **No pointer lock is needed**, which keeps the game simple inside the hub's frames. Hide the cursor during events when the mouse is in use: the faint ring on the Balance Disc shows where it is asking you to go.\n- **Which device moves your weight:** whichever was used last. With a pad, ignore the pointer. With keyboard and mouse, keys win while a weight key is down, otherwise the pointer, once it has moved. A resting mouse never fights a resting stick.\n- **Keys in balance events ✔: they move the asking ring, and it stays where you leave it.** Holding a key moves the ring at 1.6 units/s; releasing leaves it there, and C recentres. The ring must be clearly visible on the Balance Disc, because a player steering by keys watches the ring, not the dot. I tested three models with a person-like bot. Keys that spring back on release needed 9.6 presses a second just to hold a pose. A tap-then-accelerate model scored worst (Poses Tier 1: 68 / 75 / 38). This one holds up against the stick: Poses Tier 1 98 / 62 / 54 (stick 100 / 76 / 63), Tier 2 86 / 58 / 34 (90 / 64 / 53), Still Water Tier 1 100 / 85 / 28 (100 / 93 / 24), Fruit Hat Tier 1 21 / 22 / 20 banked with no falls (28 / 33 / 28), Room Service Tier 1 all five glasses. Use a move speed of about 1.6 and don't add acceleration.\n- **Keys when you're moving, not balancing: direct.** Walking on the island, Tippy Raft, steering in Island Loop and spinning in Springboard's air use the raw key direction, as PC players expect. Plate Spinners has its own scheme ✔, below.\n- **Foot keys** ramp 0 to 1 in 0.12 s.\n- **Latch presses ✔.** A key or mouse button pressed and released between two input polls must still count, or quick taps vanish at low frame rates. Normalise key codes too: the hub's harness sends synthetic events whose `code` is just `a`, so accept `a` and `KeyA` alike.\n- **Read mouse buttons from the `buttons` bitmask ✔.** Pointer Events don't fire `pointerdown` for a second button while one is already held, so with left and right as the two feet, \"both buttons\" (Hop, Log Reps, Springboard) never registers if you only listen for `pointerdown`. Read `e.buttons` on down, up and move: bit 1 is left, 2 is right, 4 is middle.\n- **Hover means the pointer moved ✔.** Move focus only on a `pointermove` whose coordinates changed. A browser also fires `pointerenter` when a menu appears under a parked mouse, and that made focus jump by itself under a controller player.\n- **On the island,** a hovered thing (pavilion, jar, board, Trudy) gets a thick outline and a label. Left click walks there and uses it. WASD and E work without the mouse. Hit-test by projecting each hotspot's anchor to screen and taking the nearest within a radius. It is simple and robust.\n- **Prompts follow the last input.** Track `lastDevice` (`pad` or `kbm`): a pad counts when a stick passes 0.25 or a button is pressed; keyboard and mouse count on keydown, mousedown or 4+ px of movement. Prompts re-render the same frame. A Settings option switches pad glyphs between PlayStation shapes and Xbox letters (A B X Y, LT RT LB RB). Keyboard prompts show keycaps, mouse prompts show a mouse with the button lit. Draw all glyphs as inline SVG in code. Every glyph element carries a `data-glyph` attribute that tests can read.\n- **On the island,** Cross and E use the focused thing if there is one; otherwise Cross and Space hop.\n- **Create + Options belongs to the hub.** Ignore Options while Create (button 8) is held, everywhere.\n- **If a player's pad disconnects mid-class,** pause and say so on screen.\n- **Haptics:** `pad.vibrationActuator?.playEffect('dual-rumble', …)` on falls, catches, rising fatigue and near-tip. Guard it, with a Settings toggle.\n- **Audio on a controller ✔.** Call `AudioContext.resume()` on pad input too, not only on keys and clicks. My first wiring left a controller-only player in silence. I can't confirm here that every browser accepts a gamepad press as the gesture that unlocks audio, so if the context is still suspended a few seconds after pad input, show a small tag asking for a key or click.\n\n---\n\n## 4. The balance model\n\nThis is the heart of the game and it is the most tested part of this plan. Copy it, then tune it.\n\n**The doll's \"balance core\"** is one Rapier dynamic body on a ball joint at the foot. Everything in the game that stands uses it. Its colliders can carry props, and those props push back on it through real contacts, so a heavy hat makes you top-heavy without any special code.\n\n```js\n// m = 1, h = 0.75 (foot to centre of mass), doll stands 1.5 m tall. Fixed step 1/120 s.\nconst body = world.createRigidBody(RAPIER.RigidBodyDesc.dynamic()\n  .setTranslation(fx, fy + 0.75, fz).setCanSleep(false).setAdditionalSolverIterations(4));\nworld.createCollider(RAPIER.ColliderDesc.capsule(0.5, 0.25).setMass(1).setCollisionGroups(DOLL(lane)), body);\nconst joint = world.createImpulseJoint(\n  RAPIER.JointData.spherical({x:0,y:0,z:0}, {x:0,y:-0.75,z:0}), footBody, body, true);\njoint.setContactsEnabled(false);            // footBody is kinematicPositionBased\n\nconst G = 9.81 * 0.75, I = 0.75 * 0.75 + 0.06;   // gravity moment, inertia about the foot\nconst K = G * (1 + margin), wn = Math.sqrt((K - G) / I), D = 2 * zeta * wn * I;\n\n// Each fixed step, before world.step():\nconst r = 1 - Math.exp(-reflex * dt);\nwx += (wishX - wx) * r;  wz += (wishZ - wz) * r;       // wish = the input, on the unit disc\nconst tx = wx * maxLean, tz = wz * maxLean;            // target lean in radians\nconst q = body.rotation();\nconst ux = 2*(q.x*q.y - q.w*q.z), uy = 1 - 2*(q.x*q.x + q.z*q.z), uz = 2*(q.y*q.z + q.w*q.x);\nleanX = Math.atan2(ux, uy);  leanZ = Math.atan2(uz, uy);   // lean toward +x and +z\nconst w = body.angvel();\nlet Tx = -(G*Math.sin(tx) + K*(leanX - tx)) + D*w.z;    // gravity feed-forward, spring, damper\nlet Tz = -(G*Math.sin(tz) + K*(leanZ - tz)) - D*w.x;\nconst limit = ankleMax * (flailLeft > 0 ? flailBoost : 1);\nconst m = Math.hypot(Tx, Tz);  if (m > limit) { Tx *= limit/m; Tz *= limit/m; }\n// nx, nz are the disturbances (sway, tremor, gusts, shoves), in the same lean space, not clamped\nbody.resetTorques(true);\nbody.addTorque({ x: Tz + nz, y: -w.y * 0.6, z: -(Tx + nx) }, true);\n```\n\n**Defaults ✔:** `maxLean` 18°, `margin` 0.45, `zeta` 0.40, `ankleMax` 4.6 N·m, `reflex` 10 /s, `flailBoost` 1.9, flail lasts 0.35 s with a 2 s cooldown, fall at 45° of lean.\n\n**What those numbers do ✔:**\n- Full stick from rest reaches 90% in about 1 s, overshoots to about 23° and settles at 18°. It is a soft, wobbly, toy-like feel.\n- Whipping the stick end to end at about 0.5 Hz survives. Slamming it from −18° to +18° peaks near 28° at `ankleMax` 4.2 and up, and falls at 3.8. This is why careful correction matters.\n- The torque limit makes a natural point of no return at `asin(ankleMax / G)`, about 39° with no load. Hat and stone loads lower it automatically.\n\n**Gusts at the defaults ✔ (0.5 s long; these numbers are the difficulty dial):**\n\n| Gust | Do nothing | Flail as you pass about 30° | Stick against it once it has started | Braced: stick at 60% against it, 0.5 s before it hits |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2 N·m | safe, peaks 22° | | | |\n| 2.5 N·m | safe, peaks 31° | | | |\n| 3 N·m | **falls** | saved, peaks 38° | falls | safe, peaks 19° |\n| 3.5 N·m | falls | saved only if timed near 32°, peaks 43° | falls | safe, peaks 29° |\n| 4 N·m | falls | falls | falls | falls |\n\nSo a telegraph is worth a lot: **give a warning before every gust of 3 N·m or more.** And flail is a real last-moment save with timing in it: a flail used early, before you have moved much, wears off and you still fall. It is the moment people will shout at the TV.\n\n**The load rule ✔.** Every prop adds a gravity moment δ = mass × 9.81 × height. The doll grows unstable when total δ exceeds K − G (3.3 at the default margin). A prop's mass therefore decides how many you can carry. Too heavy and the doll tips over at once: with margin 0.45, 0.06 kg fruit tipped it at the 5th or 6th catch, while 0.015 kg works out to about 13. **Keep prop masses small** (0.01 to 0.03 kg) against the doll's 1 kg.\n\n**Slow sway is what makes holding still a game ✔.** A thumb is far steadier than a body on a board, so a player who simply parks the stick on a target and leaves it scored 100% in my first test. Fast tremor barely moves the dot. The fix is slow forcing that the player has to see and counter by hand. Add it to `nx`, `nz`:\n\n```js\nnx = sway  *(Math.sin(t*0.9 + ph)*0.6 + Math.sin(t*1.73 + ph*2.1)*0.4)\n   + tremor*(Math.sin(t*8.2 + 1.1)*0.6 + Math.sin(t*18.3)*0.4);\nnz = sway  *(Math.sin(t*0.77 + ph*1.7)*0.6 + Math.sin(t*1.51 + ph*0.6)*0.4)\n   + tremor*(Math.sin(t*7.1 + 2.3)*0.6 + Math.sin(t*15.7 + 0.7)*0.4);   // ph from the event's seed\n```\nSway units are N·m. Sway of 0.2 moves the dot about a quarter of the disc if ignored. Tremor is mostly for looks and rumble.\n\n**Traps I hit ✔:**\n- **The doll's colliders must not touch the ground.** The capsule's foot rotating into the floor silently limits lean to about 8° and every event feels dead. Use collision groups: `(membership << 16) | filter`. Bits: `GROUND 0x0001`, props of lane *i* `0x0010 << i`, doll of lane *i* `0x0100 << i`, ragdoll parts `0x1000`. A doll meets only its own lane's props. A prop meets its own lane's doll, its own lane's props and the ground. Ragdoll parts meet only the ground. Lanes never touch each other.\n- **Stacked things use cuboid colliders.** Convex-hull stones collapsed by themselves with no disturbance at all (5th stone, every seed). Plain cuboids held. Draw a rounded stone over a box collider.\n- **Foot motion must be smooth.** With a kinematic foot bobbing 3 cm, or moving in stop-start strides, everything left the tray. Steps set a target speed and the foot low-passes toward it. Bob the visual only.\n- Use `world.integrationParameters.numSolverIterations = 8` to `10` for events with props.\n- **A Rapier panic kills the physics for the whole page ✔.** It surfaces as `RuntimeError: unreachable` from the WASM module, and no later call recovers. I hit two causes, both of them easy to write by accident:\n  1. **Touching a body after removing it, or removing it twice.** Drop every reference when you remove a body: remove it from your lists in the same line.\n  2. **Removing a prop and the collider it rested on in the same step, or the support first and the prop later.** `world.step()` panics about half a second later, once the body moves. Even one stone does it. It looks fine if nothing moves, so a quick test can miss it. **Safe: remove the prop, step once, then remove the support.** `collider.setEnabled(false)` and `body.setEnabled(false)` are safe too, and so is freeing the whole world.\n  Everything else the plan does soaked clean ✔: ragdoll spawn and remove 30 times with the doll swaying, four ragdolls at once, fruit loaded and removed 40 times, removing and re-creating the foot joint 30 times, plate shards 40 times, tumblers respawned 30 times, 60 fresh worlds in a row, props removed on a gravity-tilted island. If a panic still happens: out of a session, save and reload the page; in a session, call `AIHub.fail(message)`. Never catch it and carry on.\n\n**The Balance Disc.** `cob = (leanX, leanZ) / maxLean` is the dot, shown truthfully. Draw it as inline SVG and move it with transforms each frame. It is the game's signature HUD: a large circular dial with rings, a target zone that turns green when you are inside, the solid dot, a faint ring showing where your stick is asking to go, and a short inked trail of the last half second, like a seismograph needle. Diameter is about 22% of screen height, bottom left. Trudy peeks over its rim and reacts. In Group Class each player gets a smaller one (14%) along the bottom edge.\n\n**Falling.** At 45° of lean the doll becomes a ragdoll (section 10) with a clatter of wooden clacks, a quip, a rumble and a comic \"CLACK!\" word in the world. After `recoverTime` (2.2 s) the parts snap back with a spring noise and the doll bounces upright. Only the current hold or rep is lost. Disable the core while the ragdoll runs (`body.setEnabled(false)`). On recovery reset its pose, velocities and **your cached `leanX`, `leanZ`** ✔: if you forget, the stale lean makes the doll fall again at once, for ever. Falls are counted in the Journal as Floor Exercises.\n\n**Flail.** One button, all devices. Arms windmill for 0.35 s and the ankle gets `flailBoost` strength. Cooldown is 2 s. It looks ridiculous on purpose. It is the save that makes near-falls exciting, and it only works if you time it (see the table above). A flail costs a small form penalty in Stillness events, and the instructor comments.\n\n**Breath.** In Stillness events a breath ring pulses: 4 s growing, 4 s shrinking. Hold Breathe while it grows, release while it shrinks. Over the last full cycle, 70%+ in sync multiplies sway and tremor by 0.6 and form gain by 1.25. Out of sync does nothing bad except miss the bonus. Accessibility toggle: auto-breathe. It is worth a lot ✔: perfectly in sync, Poses Tier 1 goes 95→100 / 66→91 / 54→78, and The Cairn Tier 2 goes from 5.3 / 4.8 / 2 stones of 8 to 8 / 8 / 6.3.\n\n**Difficulty** (Settings): Gentle, Standard (default), Honest. It multiplies `ankleMax` by 1.25 / 1 / 0.9, disturbances by 0.65 / 1 / 1.2 and rhythm windows by 1.4 / 1 / 0.8.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Programme and progress\n\n**Days are game days, not calendar days.** A day ends when you go to bed. The real date is only used for absence jokes and calendar stamps.\n\n| Day | Name | Check-In tests | Classes | What it teaches |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Arrival | Stand Still, Lean Match, Step Time | Poses, Fruit Hat | Lean, flail, breath, the jar |\n| 2 | The Second Day, Which Is Harder | Stand Still, Lean Match, Quick Lean | Log Reps, Step Class, The Cairn | Feet, rhythm, stacking |\n| 3 | Service With a Wobble | One-Foot Hold, Lean Match, Step Time | Room Service, Plate Spinners, Plank & Perch | Stepping, stick circles. The Mirror opens |\n| 4 | A Little Damp | Quick Lean, One-Foot Hold, Stand Still | Springboard, Still Water, Island Loop | Air control. Group Class invitation |\n| 5 | Level Day | Your three best tests | Tippy Raft, Tug of Peace, The Grand Hold | The ending |\n| 6+ | Maintenance | Random three | Trudy's random plan of three | Free Play continues forever |\n\nBuild the day list from the event registry: an event that was cut (section 18) is skipped and everything still works.\n\n**A day's flow:** morning line → personal question (Yes/No) → Check-In → Wobble Age reveal → Today's Plan (cards in any order: walk there or pick a card to go directly) → each class → Results → marbles → the island comes up a notch → extra Free Play if wanted → the bed hut. Press Use, the screen inks out, a stamp lands on the calendar, the next dawn. Palettes follow the day's steps (dawn, noon, golden, dusk), unless an event pins its own (Step Class at dusk, The Grand Hold at sunset).\n\n**The Check-In** (about 90 s) takes place on a round mat with Trudy. Three tests, each one screen with one big instruction. Each scores 0 to 1 (clamped):\n- **Stand Still** (10 s, sway .1, tremor .06): keep the dot in the centre. Score `1 − meanDistance / .25` ✔ .82 / .74 / .49.\n- **Lean Match** (18 s, sway .04): \"Lean 70% left.\" A bar shows your percentage. Three targets, 6 s each, ignoring the first 1.5 s of each. Score `1 − meanError / .4` ✔ .58 / .45 / .27. (My first version, hold within ±5% for 2 s, was impossible: every bot timed out.)\n- **Step Time** (16 s): alternate feet with a metronome at 96 BPM. Score `1 − meanError / 120 ms` ✔ .81 / .63 / .37.\n- **Quick Lean** (15 s): lean toward the lit quadrant, six prompts. Score `1 − (meanReaction − .35 s) / .9 s` ✔ .87 / .69 / .44.\n- **One-Foot Hold** (12 s): keep R2 down and L2 up, which halves `margin`, while keeping the dot centred. Score as Stand Still ✔ .55 / .48 / .40.\n\n**Wobble Age.** `age = round(18 + 81 × (1 − mean)^1.3 + handicap)`, clamped to 18..99. The handicap is a comic head start that shrinks each day: +24, +16, +8, +3, then 0. So the number always falls, and that is what makes coming back feel good. With unchanged skill, Day 1 to Day 5 ✔: 56→32 / 66→42 / 86→62. The reveal is the comic set piece: lights dim, drum roll, numbers spin on a wooden counter, sting, Trudy's line from a band of lines by age. Stored per day and drawn as the Journal's graph. It never asks your real age (\"I didn't ask how old you are. I asked your knees.\").\n\n**Marbles.** After each class: 2 per minute of active play (min 2) + medal bonus (1, 2, 3, 5) + 2 for a first clear. Each Check-In gives 1. They pour physically into the jar on the island, as real Rapier balls: a 200-marble pour runs fine (✔ 0.23 ms per step; 500 is 0.67 ms). Marble colour shows the medal. Thresholds unlock hats and paints (`src/data/unlocks.js`: 6, 14, 24, 36, 50, 60 marbles). A full programme pays about 15, 37, 59, 81, 103 by the end of each day, or 61 in total with all Tier B events cut, so everything unlocks either way ✔.\n\n**The island's tilt depends on progress, not skill.** Rest tilt = 9° × (1 − completed programme steps / total steps), so nobody can be stuck. Each step lowers it with a long wooden creak, all loose props shuffling and Trudy reacting. The last notch happens inside The Grand Hold.\n\n**Medals** come from form (0 to 100): ≥40 Adequate, ≥65 Pleasant, ≥85 Exemplary, ≥97 Suspicious. Below 40 is still a pass (\"Attended\"). Section 6 gives an event's form where it has its own formula. Everywhere else, form = 100 × raw / `par`, with `par` set per tier so the sharp bot lands 88 to 95 (section 17).\n\n**Tiers.** Every event has three: Gentle, Honest, Unreasonable. Tier 2 opens with a Pleasant on Tier 1, Tier 3 with an Exemplary on Tier 2. Days 1 to 5 use Tier 1. Maintenance days use the highest tier opened. Free Play offers all open tiers.\n\n**Free Play** opens each event once it has been met in the programme. Walk to its pavilion, or use the menu.\n\n**The ending.** The Grand Hold (section 6) ends with the island level. Trudy's bubble centres for the first time. She has only ever seen the world at nine degrees. \"Oh. So that's what the middle looks like.\" Silence, then the marbles flare, the lighthouse lights, and the credits roll over a slow pan of everything you straightened. Then: \"It's quite flat, isn't it? ...Same time tomorrow?\" Save the \"levelled\" state, unlock Free Play for everything, and carry on into Maintenance days.\n\n**Profiles.** Up to four, each with a name, paint, hat, days, ages, medals, marbles and counts. Name is a spinner over generated names (no text entry; `src/data/names.js`). Save JSON in `localStorage` with a version number, read inside try/catch, and fall back to memory if storage is unavailable.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Events\n\n### Common rules\n\n**What you can and can't do.** Every Intro Card and the How to Play page say what you can do here and, in one line, what you can't: \"You can't fall in this one.\" \"Your feet don't matter here.\" \"You can't lose this; you can only do it badly.\" A player should never have to guess whether a thing is possible.\n\n**Flow.** Intro Card (name, one sentence, what you can and can't do, one diagram, this device's prompts; Use to begin) → instructor counts 3-2-1 → play → whistle or bell → Results (ink-stamp medal, one big number, one line of stats, a line of dialogue, retry or continue) → marbles in the programme.\n\n**Lanes.** Every event is written for an array of lanes from the first line: `for (const lane of this.lanes)`. A lane is `{ id, kind: 'local' | 'bot' | 'ghost', input, doll, balancer, props, score, name, colour }`. Solo is one lane. Group Class is up to four local lanes. A hub session is one local lane plus ghost lanes. Attract mode uses bot lanes. Lanes sit 3.2 m apart on x. Each has its own collision bits. Ghost lanes have no physics at all (section 12). The camera frames all lanes in Group Class, and your own lane with neighbours partly visible in a session. Field of view 38° ✔ (the look tests used it). Pull back to fit lanes: `d = max(base, (spread / 2 + 2.5) / tan(hfov / 2))`. Chase camera: about (0, 3.4, 9) behind the doll, damped at 6 /s.\n\n**Each event provides:** `buildSet()`, `start()`, `fixed(dt)`, `frame(dt)`, `laneResult(i)`, `botInput(i, t, skill)`, `dispose()`, plus static `id, title, discipline, instructor, tiers, couch, session, sessionConfig`. A fresh Rapier world per event, freed in `dispose()`: no state leaks. Gameplay randomness comes only from `rng('event-id')` (section 16).\n\n**Non-standing poses reuse the balance core:** in Plank & Perch, Still Water and Tug of Peace the core stays upright and invisible, and the visible doll plays the pose and rocks with it. Nothing in the rules knows the difference.\n\n### STILLNESS, with Ms. Stillwater\n\n**Poses** (Sun Deck, a round teak deck on stilts over the water; low three-quarter camera, heron beside you)\n- *Play:* The heron demonstrates, then the target ring glides from centre to the pose's spot on the disc (3 s). Follow it, then hold while the ring shrinks. Breath ring runs throughout.\n- *Tiers ✔:* maxLean 14°, margin 0.45. Tier 1: 3 poses, hold 8 s, ring 0.30→0.16, sway 0.08→0.22, tremor 0.08→0.20. Tier 2: 4 poses, hold 10 s, ring 0.30→0.13, sway 0.12→0.30, tremor 0.10→0.30. Tier 3: 5 poses, hold 12 s, ring 0.30→0.10, sway 0.15→0.38, tremor 0.15→0.40.\n- *Scoring ✔:* each tick in the ring earns `0.30 / ringRadius`; form is the share of the maximum, capped at 100. Without breath: Tier 1 95 / 66 / 54, Tier 2 74 / 58 / 46, Tier 3 55 / 41 / 33. In sync throughout: Tier 1 100 / 91 / 78, Tier 2 100 / 85 / 71, Tier 3 96 / 62 / 57. Tiers 2 and 3 are hard on purpose, and breath is how you get there.\n- *Pose table* (`src/data/poses.js`, spot on the disc): The Contented Heron (−.35, 0), The Apologetic Pine (0, .4), Half-Folded Deck Chair (0, −.35), The Lighthouse Keeper (.4, 0), Startled Flamingo (.35, .2), The Long Goodbye (−.2, .45), Someone Else's Umbrella (.45, −.1), The Quiet Regret (−.4, −.2). All standing poses.\n- *Bot:* follow the ring with a delayed correction; breathe in sync.\n","numLines":250,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1113}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01FmkLrKN65hnTncMreQ4nxV","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
