{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgjmomc2kynnVHwLJ3u7N","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I'm partway through reading PLAN.md. It's a very large spec for a Wii Fit-style balance game, and I'm on the last sections. After that I'll set up PROGRESS.md and start building."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15254,"cache_read_input_tokens":57959,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":15254},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cc2a51a5-f08a-42e7-afc4-8d441845bc15","uuid":"ef1cde2e-b06a-481c-bb87-922fcb8ad11c","timestamp":"2026-11-05T12:22:37.181Z","request_id":"req_011CgjmomLu7QqtbkvdfT7hB"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgjmomc2kynnVHwLJ3u7N","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VAXWhVU1QmznoqTc7zY8Ya","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-052201__x29b4d9/work/PLAN.md","offset":851},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15254,"cache_read_input_tokens":57959,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":15254},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cc2a51a5-f08a-42e7-afc4-8d441845bc15","uuid":"989cf4cd-bb0b-409d-828d-4c33e76e8816","timestamp":"2026-11-05T12:22:37.839Z","request_id":"req_011CgjmomLu7QqtbkvdfT7hB","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01VAXWhVU1QmznoqTc7zY8Ya":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-052201__x29b4d9/work/PLAN.md","offset":851}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VAXWhVU1QmznoqTc7zY8Ya","type":"tool_result","content":"851\t\n852\t**The last thing you do, in this order:**\n853\t1. Finish the game and run `node tools/test.mjs full`.\n854\t2. Write the exit-interview file beside the HTML, using `notes/issues.md` for the counts.\n855\t3. Run `node tools/build.mjs`. It reads that file into `src/data/about.js`, so there is one source of truth and the three places can't disagree.\n856\t4. Run `node tools/test.mjs unit ui`. These fail if the file doesn't parse, doesn't match TASK.md's shape, or isn't on the About screen word for word.\n857\t5. Paste the same content into your final chat reply, in the format below.\n858\t\n859\tDuring development, About shows what is known so far. The final build must have no gaps: \"unknown\" is allowed only for what you truly can't confirm.\n860\t\n861\t**The About/Credits screen.** Reached from the main menu. It follows the 10-foot rules: large type, one page at a time, moved with left and right or clicks, never a scrolling wall of text. It is also the one screen where long answers may take several pages: split them rather than shrink them.\n862\t\n863\t| Page | Shows |\n864\t|---|---|\n865\t| 1 | Source Information: Date, Source, Plan, Genre and Perspective |\n866\t| 2 | Source Information: Tech, Art Direction, Animation |\n867\t| 3 | Source Information: Audio, Online, Testing |\n868\t| 4 | Source Information: Research, Environment, Model, Usage |\n869\t| 5 | Testing Feedback: the 4 questions with your answers |\n870\t| 6 | Engine Feedback: the 6 questions with your answers |\n871\t| 7 | Prompt Feedback: the 4 questions with your answers |\n872\t| 8 | Credits: Fredoka (SIL OFL), Three.js, Rapier, and the title |\n873\t\n874\tUsage must read exactly `added on import`. Keep that text as one plain string, because the agent that imports the game into AI Hub replaces it with the real cost, tokens and time. I ran this mechanism on the prototype ✔: an About screen fed from a JSON file, with a test confirming every value and answer appears on it.\n875\t\n876\t**Your final chat reply** has this shape, with your own answers:\n877\t\n878\t```\n879\t## Source Information\n880\t- Date: ...          (and so on for all 14 fields, in TASK.md's order)\n881\t## Testing Feedback\n882\t1. ...               (4 answers, in order)\n883\t## Engine Feedback\n884\t1. ...               (6 answers, in order)\n885\t## Prompt Feedback\n886\t1. ...               (4 answers, in order)\n887\t```\n888\t\n889\t**Source Information.** Field by field:\n890\t\n891\t| Field | What to write |\n892\t|---|---|\n893\t| Date | The date line in your TASK.md |\n894\t| Source | Wii Fit |\n895\t| Plan | The file name of the plan you were given |\n896\t| Genre and Perspective | Physics-balance party sports with a daily-ritual progression; third person, with framed stage cameras per event and an orbitable chase camera on the island |\n897\t| Tech | Three.js R180 on the WebGPU backend (WebGL2 fallback), TSL toon and ink post, Rapier 0.19.3, Web Audio, Gamepad API, esbuild, Fredoka, plus anything you added, with versions |\n898\t| Art Direction | Cel-shaded ink filter over a wooden-toy resort, all models made in code on Three.js's own materials |\n899\t| Animation | Three.js `AnimationMixer` clips on jointed rigs, procedural lean from the physics, Rapier ragdolls |\n900\t| Audio | Code-synthesised city-pop and bossa at 10% by default, synthesised sound effects, Animalese and Simlish-style gibberish voices; no audio files |\n901\t| Online | AI Hub Session Protocol v1 in ghost mode for 1-4 devices, plus 1-4 players on one screen |\n902\t| Testing | The parameters TASK.md allows: as much headless testing as you like, with scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game; one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, rerun only after changing the code it covers; screenshots only for what a script can't check; no subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit. Then what you actually ran |\n903\t| Research | Yours, or none, beside this plan's: the hub's protocol pages and code; background on the source from Wikipedia, a Nintendo World Report review and Nintendo's Iwata Asks interviews, read as search summaries. How much more to research is your choice |\n904\t| Environment | As written in your TASK.md |\n905\t| Model | As written in your TASK.md |\n906\t| Usage | Exactly `added on import`. You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it |\n907\t\n908\t**Testing Feedback,** answered in order (`notes/issues.md` gives you the counts):\n909\t1. How many issues did testing catch?\n910\t2. How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\n911\t3. What was the most helpful test you ran?\n912\t4. What was the least helpful test you ran?\n913\t\n914\t**Engine Feedback,** answered in order:\n915\t1. What did you like most about the engine?\n916\t2. Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\n917\t3. What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\n918\t4. What was the hardest part about using the engine?\n919\t5. What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\n920\t6. If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\n921\t\n922\t**Prompt Feedback,** answered in order:\n923\t1. What did you like most about this prompt?\n924\t2. What was confusing?\n925\t3. What was the hardest thing to do?\n926\t4. What would you change in this prompt?\n927\t\n928\t**The exit-interview file** is one JSON object in TASK.md's exact shape, plain text, every value filled in, and you check that it parses: `sourceInformation` (`date, source, plan, genre, perspective, tech, artDirection, animation, audio, online, testing, research, environment, model, usage`), `testingFeedback`, `engineFeedback`, `promptFeedback` (arrays, answers in order), and `aiHub`:\n929\t\n930\t| `aiHub` field | Suggested |\n931\t|---|---|\n932\t| `name` | The game's title, as on its title screen |\n933\t| `tagline` | A few words of your own |\n934\t| `description` | One or two sentences, such as: a wooden-toy wellness retreat on a crooked island, where you hold poses, stack stones and catch fruit with real balance physics, and straighten the whole island one class at a time |\n935\t| `type` | Game |\n936\t| `genre` | Sports |\n937\t| `source` | Wii Fit |\n938\t| `series` | Empty: the franchise is the source itself, and the hub's catalogue wants it empty then |\n939\t| `players` | 1–4 |\n940\t| `controls` | The same line as `hub:session-howto` |\n941\t| `tags` | 3 to 6 lowercase, such as 3d, comedy, third-person, versus, arcade |\n942\t\n943\t---\n944\t\n945\t## 20. What was and wasn't verified\n946\t\n947\t**Tested here on 2026-11-04 (✔ above):** versions exist on npm and jsDelivr; single-file esbuild build from `file://`; WebGPU in Chromium with SwiftShader, and which flags really render; WebGL2 fallback in Chromium and WebKit; `toonOutlinePass` with MRT and the custom ink pass; instanced toon meshes; glass; shadow settings; the look of the cast and all four palettes; client hash; balance model; torque limit; gusts and flail; island tilt; marble pours; ragdoll; Rapier's crash conditions and a soak of every removal path; touch media queries; Fredoka's licence and file names.\n948\t\n949\t**Every event's numbers were simulated with the bots,** on real Rapier where physics matter: Fruit Hat, Poses, The Cairn, Still Water, Log Reps, Plank & Perch, Tug of Peace, Room Service, Springboard, Island Loop, The Grand Hold and Step Class on the real balance core, Tippy Raft, Plate Spinners from a moving stick vector, and the Check-In. Keys were simulated against the stick for Poses, Still Water, Fruit Hat, Room Service and Plate Spinners. This overturned my first numbers for Plate Spinners, Tug of Peace, Log Reps, Plank & Perch, Room Service, Tippy Raft, Lean Match, The Cairn's top tier, Step Class's lean notes and the keyboard model. Expect the same when you build: **measure before you trust a number.**\n950\t\n951\t**An integration prototype.** Separate checks don't prove the parts work together, so I built one event through everything, from this document: its build recipe, its code blocks extracted directly from this file, a rig with `AnimationMixer` clips, ragdoll from the rig, Fruit Hat written for lanes, all three input devices, live prompts with both glyph sets, menus, pause, Dev Mode sliders with reset, attract mode, touch-only, an About screen fed from a JSON file, audio buses with the title motif, and a hub session with its own card, ghost lanes and a timeline on the hub's clock. Then I tested it the way section 17 says, with one command: **209 passed, 0 failed,** with real rendering, including all 16 harness checks in each of five hub scenarios (1, 2 and 4 players, bad network, host leaving, short limit), a wall-clock attract, three touch profiles, and the 1080p layout audit. That took about 360 lines of game code and 170 of tests, and came to a 3.1 MB single file. It is deleted now; everything it taught is in this plan. It found problems no separate check had shown: the lost device, the parked mouse stealing focus, both mouse buttons never registering together, silent controller play, the fall loop, lost quick taps, and slow time at low frame rates.\n952\t\n953\t**Not tested. TASK.md says anything needing the real thing \"is checked as far as it can be here, never required\", and these are as far as it goes:**\n954\t- **Real hardware.** Nobody has played this with a real controller on a real TV. I can't confirm that every browser accepts a gamepad press as the gesture that unlocks audio (section 3 gives the fallback).\n955\t- **Hearing.** Nobody has listened. Pitches, levels and readability are checked as signals; whether the music is good is not. Check it with the audio tests and trust the arrangement.\n956\t- **People.** The bots are a model of a person, and their timing and tracking errors are my assumptions, so skill results are estimates. Mouse play shares the stick's path, and was tested for input, not simulated for skill.\n957\t- **The whole game.** The prototype integrates one event of thirteen, with a stand-in look. The rest is simulated, not built. All animation beyond a couple of test clips, and any art beyond the cast and a test scene, is yours. Whether it is fun is yours to judge.\n958\t\n959\t---\n960\t\n961\t## 21. TASK.md coverage and proof\n962\t\n963\tEvery requirement in TASK.md, in its order: where this plan answers it, and what proves it was met when you build. Test groups are in section 17. Some of what TASK.md asks for can only exist once there is a build: the game itself, its About/Credits screen, the exit-interview file and your final report. This plan covers those by saying exactly what to produce, when, and how it gets checked. Producing them is the build's job, and yours.\n964\t\n965\t| TASK.md asks for | Answered in | Proven at build time by |\n966\t|---|---|---|\n967\t| Source: Wii Fit | Section 1, what we keep; section 2, what we don't copy | `unit`: source's names absent outside the title and About. Your art pass |\n968\t| Genre, picked | Section 1 | Your report, section 19 |\n969\t| Perspective, picked | Section 1; each event's camera in section 6 | Art pass screenshots |\n970\t| Title: a riff, 50 brainstormed, picked with a real random number generator | Left to you, as this plan's brief says: section 1 | You run the generator; one constant in `src/data/config.js` |\n971\t| Research, your choice | Section 19, Research row | Your report |\n972\t| Package: one HTML file; downloadable dependencies optional | Section 16, Build | `unit`: inline scripts only. `flow`: opens from `file://` |\n973\t| Three.js R180 | Section 16, Stack | `flow`: `THREE.REVISION` is 180 ✔ |\n974\t| WebGPU | Section 16, Renderer | `events`: `backend` is webgpu and `render.lost` is null ✔ |\n975\t| Stylized cel-shaded / ink filter | Section 9 | `events`: ink pixels in a read-back screenshot ✔. Art pass |\n976\t| Rapier 0.19.3 | Sections 4 and 16 | `flow`: `RAPIER.version()` ✔ |\n977\t| Web Audio | Section 11 | `audio`: live bus and offline renders ✔ |\n978\t| Gamepad API | Section 3 | `input`: every verb through the stubbed pad ✔ |\n979\t| Rest of the stack is yours | Section 16: esbuild, Playwright, Fredoka | Your report |\n980\t| Art Direction: engine's style as base, new assets, library for generic props | Section 9, Base style and Model recipes | Art pass |\n981\t| Animation: engine's system as base, new animations | Section 10 | `events`: the mixer's time advances and clips change ✔ |\n982\t| Audio: all in code, no files | Section 11 | `unit`: greps. `audio`: every track, sting, effect and voice renders non-silent |\n983\t| Voices: Animalese / Simlish-style gibberish | Section 11, Voices | `audio`: blips per line, peaks balanced |\n984\t| Music at 10% by default | Sections 7 and 11 | `audio`: setting 10, bus gain .10 ✔ |\n985\t| Online: AI Hub | Section 12 | `hub` ✔ |\n986\t| Subfolder for dev files; modules bundled to one file in root | Section 16: file tree and Build | The build writes where TASK.md says; `unit` opens it |\n987\t| External CDN allowed | Section 16: not needed, everything is inlined | `unit`: no external scripts |\n988\t| Don't read outside your folder; shared ports and processes | Section 17, Sharing this computer | Your practice |\n989\t| Prefer `file://` and headless tools; 127.0.0.1 on an OS-picked port; confirm the page; stop only your own PIDs | Section 17, Sharing this computer; `hub` test | `hub`: in-process server on port 0, page confirmed first ✔ |\n990\t| Performance budget unlimited; FPS and optimisation out of scope | Section 9, Detail; section 16, Renderer | Nothing to prove: no FPS test exists on purpose |\n991\t| Testing: scripts that play and check state, scripted whole-game playthrough | Section 17: test hooks, `flow` test | `flow` reaches the ending and beyond |\n992\t| One command, only failures and a one-line count, rerun only after changes | Section 17, The command | Its own output ✔ |\n993\t| Screenshots only for what a script can't check | Section 17: art pass | Your practice |\n994\t| No subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit | Section 17, first paragraph | Your practice |\n995\t| Effort, your choice | Section 18 gives an order; how far you take it is yours | Your report |\n996\t| This computer: tools, browsers, WebGPU flags and secure pages | Sections 16 and 17, Browser flags | `events`: `render.lost` null ✔ |\n997\t| Display, Firefox, WebKit, blank WebGPU screenshots | Section 17, Browser flags | `events`: WebKit boot ✔ |\n998\t| Memory and earlyoom | Section 17, Sharing this computer | Your practice |\n999\t| No controller, touch, listener, second player, iPhone or TV: test with the stand-ins, \"never required\" to have the real thing | Section 17, Stand-ins: these stand-ins are what TASK.md asks for | `input`, `ui`, `audio`, `hub` ✔ |\n1000\t| Sharing: browsers, `/tmp`, `playwright install`, system Python and `uv` | Section 17, Sharing this computer | Your practice |\n1001\t| Original game inspired by the source | Sections 1 and 2 | `unit` greps; your art pass and judgement |\n1002\t| Feature-complete demo, polished vertical slice, long enough to show everything | Section 1, Scope; section 5, the day table; section 18 | `flow`: every day and event, in order |\n1003\t| Capture the source's world, characters, themes and tone in mechanics, pacing and UX | Section 1's table; sections 4, 5 and 8 | `balance` for mechanics; your judgement for tone |\n1004\t| Infrastructure so the rest can be built with normal designer tools | Section 14, For later designers; `src/data/` in section 16 | `unit`: table integrity; a removed event still leaves a working programme |\n1005\t| Dev mode from the pause menu, standard tools | Section 14; Pause in section 7 | `flow`: only reachable from Pause ✔ |\n1006\t| Live sliders for the key variables, reset to defaults | Section 14 | `flow`: a slider changes the running event, reset restores it ✔ |\n1007\t| Attract mode: 10 s idle on title or menu, looping AI demo, any input exits, may cheat, shows as much as possible | Section 13 | `flow`: starts near 10 wall-clock seconds, loops, each device exits it and the press is swallowed ✔ |\n1008\t| Multiplayer: hub integration | Section 12, AI Hub session | `hub`: harness at 1, 2, 4 players, bad network, host leaving ✔ |\n1009\t| Multiplayer: the game on its own | Section 12, Group Class and On its own | `input`: four pads, four lanes ✔. `hub`: Enter and Space on its own ✔ |\n1010\t| Narrative tone like the source's | Section 8 | `unit`: lines exist, no repeats. Your judgement |\n1011\t| 10-foot, controller-first, DualSense or any standard pad | Sections 3 and 7 | `ui` at 1080p ✔. `input`: all menus by pad alone ✔ |\n1012\t| Keyboard and mouse: same game, same screens, native, expected keys | Section 3 | `input`: same effects from keys and mouse ✔. `balance` on keys |\n1013\t| Mouse aims, looks, clicks menus and world things; hover moves the focus | Section 3 | `input`: pointer weight, hover, click ✔; a parked mouse doesn't steal focus ✔ |\n1014\t| Prompts follow last input; PlayStation glyphs; Xbox option; keys and mouse buttons | Section 3 | `input`: `data-glyph` changes in the same tick ✔ |\n1015\t| No dense, web-style data screens | Section 7, The 10-foot rules | `ui`: 30 px minimum, six choices, safe area, on every screen ✔ |\n1016\t| Touch-only: no touch controls, \"Controller or keyboard required\", Settings, attract runs | Section 15 | `ui`: Pixel 7, iPhone, iPad ✔ |\n1017\t| Done: no TODOs, placeholders or stubs | Sections 18 and 19 | `unit`: greps ✔ |\n1018\t| Source Information, all 14 fields, in chat and About/Credits | Section 19: field by field, the About pages, the chat format, and the order to do it in | `ui`: About shows every value from the file ✔. `unit`: the file's shape. Your chat reply |\n1019\t| Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback, in chat and About/Credits | Section 19: every question listed, the About pages, the chat format; section 17's tally for the counts | `ui`: About shows every answer ✔. `unit`: 4, 6 and 4 answers. Your chat reply |\n1020\t| Exit-interview file: beside the HTML, exact shape, plain text, parses, `aiHub` block | Section 19: what to write and when; shape in section 22 | `unit`: parses, exact keys, every value filled |\n1021\t\n1022\t✔ here means the check was run on the integration prototype of section 20.\n1023\t\n1024\t---\n1025\t\n1026\t## 22. Planning-stage report\n1027\t\n1028\tTASK.md's reporting belongs to whoever builds the game, and only your answers count for your build (section 19 says how). Here is the same report for this planning stage, so you can see the format filled in and what was learned.\n1029\t\n1030\t**Source Information.** Date: 2026-11-04 21:19:05. Source: Wii Fit. Plan: none (this stage wrote it). Genre and Perspective: physics-balance party sports with a daily-ritual progression; third person, with framed stage cameras per event and an orbitable chase camera on the island. Tech: Three.js R180 (WebGPU, WebGL2 fallback, TSL toon and ink), Rapier 0.19.3, Web Audio, Gamepad API, esbuild 0.28.2, Playwright 1.63.0, Fredoka 5.3.0. Art Direction: cel-shaded ink over a wooden-toy resort, all models made in code. Animation: `AnimationMixer` clips on jointed rigs, physics-driven lean, Rapier ragdolls (prototyped with test clips). Audio: code-synthesised city-pop and bossa at 10% by default, effects, Animalese and Simlish-style voices (motif, voices and click prototyped). Online: AI Hub Session Protocol v1 in ghost mode for 1-4 devices, plus 1-4 on one screen. Testing: as much headless testing as wanted, with scripts that play and check state; one command printing only failures and a one-line count, rerun only after changes; screenshots only for what a script can't check; no subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit. Ran: simulations with three bot skills, and the prototype's suite. Research: the hub's protocol pages and code; source background from search summaries of Wikipedia, a Nintendo World Report review and Iwata Asks. Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux. Model: Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort max. Usage: added on import.\n1031\t\n1032\t**Testing Feedback**\n1033\t1. *How many issues did testing catch?* About 39 across the prototypes and simulations.\n1034\t2. *How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?* 13: ground contact capping lean; a parked stick scoring 100%; Plate Spinners unlosable; Plate Spinners unreachable on keys; Tug of Peace unwinnable; Tippy Raft never ending; Lean Match impossible; the fall loop after a ragdoll; silent controller play; both mouse buttons never registering together; Rapier's page-killing panic; tests \"passing\" on a lost WebGPU device; a boot snippet that wouldn't build.\n1035\t3. *What was the most helpful test you ran?* Building one event through everything from the plan's own recipes. It found the lost device, the parked mouse, the mouse-button chord, silent pad play and the fall loop, none of which any separate check had shown.\n1036\t4. *What was the least helpful test you ran?* The Firefox WebGL2 checks, which changed nothing in the game.\n1037\t\n1038\t**Engine Feedback** (Three.js and Rapier, from the prototypes)\n1039\t1. *What did you like most about the engine?* `toonOutlinePass` plus a short TSL pass gave the whole ink look, unchanged on WebGPU and WebGL2.\n1040\t2. *Did you get stuck or guess wrong?* Yes. I took a lost device for 60 fps. `hash()` given a vec2 streaked the image, toon glass turned smoky, and convex-hull stones collapsed on their own.\n1041\t3. *What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?* The balance core: a torque-limited controller with gravity feed-forward on a ball joint.\n1042\t4. *What was the hardest part about using the engine?* Knowing what was really happening. A lost WebGPU device gives no error and a loop that seems to run. A Rapier crash surfaces half a second after its cause, only once something moves.\n1043\t5. *What did you change in the engine, and why?* None.\n1044\t6. *If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?* Make Rapier's panic on a stale handle or a bad removal a catchable error, instead of killing all physics for the page.\n1045\t\n1046\t**Prompt Feedback**\n1047\t1. *What did you like most about this prompt?* Open creative choices combined with exact, checkable facts about the machine and the hub.\n1048\t2. *What was confusing?* What \"the game on its own\" means for multiplayer; \"native style\" and \"asset library\" for an engine with neither; and which parts of TASK.md apply at the plan stage.\n1049\t3. *What was the hardest thing to do?* Making a balance-board game that a steady thumb can't trivially beat, while staying original.\n1050\t4. *What would you change in this prompt?* Fix the WebGPU flags, which lose the device within a second. Define standalone multiplayer. State how capable builders are.\n1051\t\n1052\t**The exit-interview file's shape,** filled in for this stage. It parses, and its keys match TASK.md's exactly. Replace every value with your own. `name` and `tagline` are \"unknown\" here only because each builder picks its own title.\n1053\t\n1054\t```json\n1055\t{\n1056\t  \"sourceInformation\": {\n1057\t    \"date\": \"2026-11-04 21:19:05\",\n1058\t    \"source\": \"Wii Fit\",\n1059\t    \"plan\": \"none\",\n1060\t    \"genre\": \"Physics-balance party sports with a daily-ritual progression\",\n1061\t    \"perspective\": \"Third person: framed stage cameras per event, orbitable chase camera on the island\",\n1062\t    \"tech\": \"Three.js R180 (WebGPU, WebGL2 fallback, TSL toon and ink), Rapier 0.19.3, Web Audio, Gamepad API, esbuild 0.28.2, Playwright 1.63.0, Fredoka 5.3.0\",\n1063\t    \"artDirection\": \"Cel-shaded ink over a wooden-toy resort, all models made in code\",\n1064\t    \"animation\": \"AnimationMixer clips on jointed rigs, physics-driven lean, Rapier ragdolls\",\n1065\t    \"audio\": \"Code-synthesised city-pop and bossa at 10% by default, effects, Animalese and Simlish-style voices\",\n1066\t    \"online\": \"AI Hub Session Protocol v1 in ghost mode for 1-4 devices, plus 1-4 on one screen\",\n1067\t    \"testing\": \"Headless scripts that play and check state, one command printing only failures and a count, screenshots only where needed, no subagents or workflows to review\",\n1068\t    \"research\": \"Hub protocol pages and code; source background from search summaries\",\n1069\t    \"environment\": \"Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\",\n1070\t    \"model\": \"Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort max\",\n1071\t    \"usage\": \"added on import\"\n1072\t  },\n1073\t  \"testingFeedback\": [\n1074\t    \"About 39 across the prototypes and simulations.\",\n1075\t    \"13.\",\n1076\t    \"Building one event through everything from the plan's own recipes.\",\n1077\t    \"The Firefox WebGL2 checks.\"\n1078\t  ],\n1079\t  \"engineFeedback\": [\n1080\t    \"toonOutlinePass plus a short TSL pass gave the whole ink look on both backends.\",\n1081\t    \"Yes. I took a lost device for 60 fps.\",\n1082\t    \"The balance core.\",\n1083\t    \"Knowing what was really happening: a lost device gives no error.\",\n1084\t    \"None.\",\n1085\t    \"Make Rapier's panic a catchable error.\"\n1086\t  ],\n1087\t  \"promptFeedback\": [\n1088\t    \"Open creative choices combined with exact, checkable facts.\",\n1089\t    \"What the game on its own means for multiplayer.\",\n1090\t    \"Making a balance-board game a steady thumb can't trivially beat.\",\n1091\t    \"Fix the WebGPU flags, which lose the device within a second.\"\n1092\t  ],\n1093\t  \"aiHub\": {\n1094\t    \"name\": \"unknown\",\n1095\t    \"tagline\": \"unknown\",\n1096\t    \"description\": \"A wooden-toy wellness retreat on a crooked island, where you hold poses, stack stones and catch fruit with real balance physics, and straighten the whole island one class at a time.\",\n1097\t    \"type\": \"Game\",\n1098\t    \"genre\": \"Sports\",\n1099\t    \"source\": \"Wii Fit\",\n1100\t    \"series\": \"\",\n1101\t    \"players\": \"1–4\",\n1102\t    \"controls\": \"Lean: stick, WASD or mouse. Feet: L2 R2, Q E or clicks. Flail: Square or F\",\n1103\t    \"tags\": [\n1104\t      \"3d\",\n1105\t      \"comedy\",\n1106\t      \"third-person\",\n1107\t      \"versus\",\n1108\t      \"arcade\"\n1109\t    ]\n1110\t  }\n1111\t}\n1112\t```\n1113\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cc2a51a5-f08a-42e7-afc4-8d441845bc15","uuid":"55cf7a8a-b743-4ba7-9455-065c8efd4085","timestamp":"2026-11-05T12:22:37.860Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-sonnet-5-5-medium.build-v2__20261105-052201__x29b4d9/work/PLAN.md","content":"\n**The last thing you do, in this order:**\n1. Finish the game and run `node tools/test.mjs full`.\n2. Write the exit-interview file beside the HTML, using `notes/issues.md` for the counts.\n3. Run `node tools/build.mjs`. It reads that file into `src/data/about.js`, so there is one source of truth and the three places can't disagree.\n4. Run `node tools/test.mjs unit ui`. These fail if the file doesn't parse, doesn't match TASK.md's shape, or isn't on the About screen word for word.\n5. Paste the same content into your final chat reply, in the format below.\n\nDuring development, About shows what is known so far. The final build must have no gaps: \"unknown\" is allowed only for what you truly can't confirm.\n\n**The About/Credits screen.** Reached from the main menu. It follows the 10-foot rules: large type, one page at a time, moved with left and right or clicks, never a scrolling wall of text. It is also the one screen where long answers may take several pages: split them rather than shrink them.\n\n| Page | Shows |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Source Information: Date, Source, Plan, Genre and Perspective |\n| 2 | Source Information: Tech, Art Direction, Animation |\n| 3 | Source Information: Audio, Online, Testing |\n| 4 | Source Information: Research, Environment, Model, Usage |\n| 5 | Testing Feedback: the 4 questions with your answers |\n| 6 | Engine Feedback: the 6 questions with your answers |\n| 7 | Prompt Feedback: the 4 questions with your answers |\n| 8 | Credits: Fredoka (SIL OFL), Three.js, Rapier, and the title |\n\nUsage must read exactly `added on import`. Keep that text as one plain string, because the agent that imports the game into AI Hub replaces it with the real cost, tokens and time. I ran this mechanism on the prototype ✔: an About screen fed from a JSON file, with a test confirming every value and answer appears on it.\n\n**Your final chat reply** has this shape, with your own answers:\n\n```\n## Source Information\n- Date: ...          (and so on for all 14 fields, in TASK.md's order)\n## Testing Feedback\n1. ...               (4 answers, in order)\n## Engine Feedback\n1. ...               (6 answers, in order)\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. ...               (4 answers, in order)\n```\n\n**Source Information.** Field by field:\n\n| Field | What to write |\n|---|---|\n| Date | The date line in your TASK.md |\n| Source | Wii Fit |\n| Plan | The file name of the plan you were given |\n| Genre and Perspective | Physics-balance party sports with a daily-ritual progression; third person, with framed stage cameras per event and an orbitable chase camera on the island |\n| Tech | Three.js R180 on the WebGPU backend (WebGL2 fallback), TSL toon and ink post, Rapier 0.19.3, Web Audio, Gamepad API, esbuild, Fredoka, plus anything you added, with versions |\n| Art Direction | Cel-shaded ink filter over a wooden-toy resort, all models made in code on Three.js's own materials |\n| Animation | Three.js `AnimationMixer` clips on jointed rigs, procedural lean from the physics, Rapier ragdolls |\n| Audio | Code-synthesised city-pop and bossa at 10% by default, synthesised sound effects, Animalese and Simlish-style gibberish voices; no audio files |\n| Online | AI Hub Session Protocol v1 in ghost mode for 1-4 devices, plus 1-4 players on one screen |\n| Testing | The parameters TASK.md allows: as much headless testing as you like, with scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game; one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, rerun only after changing the code it covers; screenshots only for what a script can't check; no subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit. Then what you actually ran |\n| Research | Yours, or none, beside this plan's: the hub's protocol pages and code; background on the source from Wikipedia, a Nintendo World Report review and Nintendo's Iwata Asks interviews, read as search summaries. How much more to research is your choice |\n| Environment | As written in your TASK.md |\n| Model | As written in your TASK.md |\n| Usage | Exactly `added on import`. You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it |\n\n**Testing Feedback,** answered in order (`notes/issues.md` gives you the counts):\n1. How many issues did testing catch?\n2. How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\n3. What was the most helpful test you ran?\n4. What was the least helpful test you ran?\n\n**Engine Feedback,** answered in order:\n1. What did you like most about the engine?\n2. Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\n3. What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\n4. What was the hardest part about using the engine?\n5. What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\n6. If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\n\n**Prompt Feedback,** answered in order:\n1. What did you like most about this prompt?\n2. What was confusing?\n3. What was the hardest thing to do?\n4. What would you change in this prompt?\n\n**The exit-interview file** is one JSON object in TASK.md's exact shape, plain text, every value filled in, and you check that it parses: `sourceInformation` (`date, source, plan, genre, perspective, tech, artDirection, animation, audio, online, testing, research, environment, model, usage`), `testingFeedback`, `engineFeedback`, `promptFeedback` (arrays, answers in order), and `aiHub`:\n\n| `aiHub` field | Suggested |\n|---|---|\n| `name` | The game's title, as on its title screen |\n| `tagline` | A few words of your own |\n| `description` | One or two sentences, such as: a wooden-toy wellness retreat on a crooked island, where you hold poses, stack stones and catch fruit with real balance physics, and straighten the whole island one class at a time |\n| `type` | Game |\n| `genre` | Sports |\n| `source` | Wii Fit |\n| `series` | Empty: the franchise is the source itself, and the hub's catalogue wants it empty then |\n| `players` | 1–4 |\n| `controls` | The same line as `hub:session-howto` |\n| `tags` | 3 to 6 lowercase, such as 3d, comedy, third-person, versus, arcade |\n\n---\n\n## 20. What was and wasn't verified\n\n**Tested here on 2026-11-04 (✔ above):** versions exist on npm and jsDelivr; single-file esbuild build from `file://`; WebGPU in Chromium with SwiftShader, and which flags really render; WebGL2 fallback in Chromium and WebKit; `toonOutlinePass` with MRT and the custom ink pass; instanced toon meshes; glass; shadow settings; the look of the cast and all four palettes; client hash; balance model; torque limit; gusts and flail; island tilt; marble pours; ragdoll; Rapier's crash conditions and a soak of every removal path; touch media queries; Fredoka's licence and file names.\n\n**Every event's numbers were simulated with the bots,** on real Rapier where physics matter: Fruit Hat, Poses, The Cairn, Still Water, Log Reps, Plank & Perch, Tug of Peace, Room Service, Springboard, Island Loop, The Grand Hold and Step Class on the real balance core, Tippy Raft, Plate Spinners from a moving stick vector, and the Check-In. Keys were simulated against the stick for Poses, Still Water, Fruit Hat, Room Service and Plate Spinners. This overturned my first numbers for Plate Spinners, Tug of Peace, Log Reps, Plank & Perch, Room Service, Tippy Raft, Lean Match, The Cairn's top tier, Step Class's lean notes and the keyboard model. Expect the same when you build: **measure before you trust a number.**\n\n**An integration prototype.** Separate checks don't prove the parts work together, so I built one event through everything, from this document: its build recipe, its code blocks extracted directly from this file, a rig with `AnimationMixer` clips, ragdoll from the rig, Fruit Hat written for lanes, all three input devices, live prompts with both glyph sets, menus, pause, Dev Mode sliders with reset, attract mode, touch-only, an About screen fed from a JSON file, audio buses with the title motif, and a hub session with its own card, ghost lanes and a timeline on the hub's clock. Then I tested it the way section 17 says, with one command: **209 passed, 0 failed,** with real rendering, including all 16 harness checks in each of five hub scenarios (1, 2 and 4 players, bad network, host leaving, short limit), a wall-clock attract, three touch profiles, and the 1080p layout audit. That took about 360 lines of game code and 170 of tests, and came to a 3.1 MB single file. It is deleted now; everything it taught is in this plan. It found problems no separate check had shown: the lost device, the parked mouse stealing focus, both mouse buttons never registering together, silent controller play, the fall loop, lost quick taps, and slow time at low frame rates.\n\n**Not tested. TASK.md says anything needing the real thing \"is checked as far as it can be here, never required\", and these are as far as it goes:**\n- **Real hardware.** Nobody has played this with a real controller on a real TV. I can't confirm that every browser accepts a gamepad press as the gesture that unlocks audio (section 3 gives the fallback).\n- **Hearing.** Nobody has listened. Pitches, levels and readability are checked as signals; whether the music is good is not. Check it with the audio tests and trust the arrangement.\n- **People.** The bots are a model of a person, and their timing and tracking errors are my assumptions, so skill results are estimates. Mouse play shares the stick's path, and was tested for input, not simulated for skill.\n- **The whole game.** The prototype integrates one event of thirteen, with a stand-in look. The rest is simulated, not built. All animation beyond a couple of test clips, and any art beyond the cast and a test scene, is yours. Whether it is fun is yours to judge.\n\n---\n\n## 21. TASK.md coverage and proof\n\nEvery requirement in TASK.md, in its order: where this plan answers it, and what proves it was met when you build. Test groups are in section 17. Some of what TASK.md asks for can only exist once there is a build: the game itself, its About/Credits screen, the exit-interview file and your final report. This plan covers those by saying exactly what to produce, when, and how it gets checked. Producing them is the build's job, and yours.\n\n| TASK.md asks for | Answered in | Proven at build time by |\n|---|---|---|\n| Source: Wii Fit | Section 1, what we keep; section 2, what we don't copy | `unit`: source's names absent outside the title and About. Your art pass |\n| Genre, picked | Section 1 | Your report, section 19 |\n| Perspective, picked | Section 1; each event's camera in section 6 | Art pass screenshots |\n| Title: a riff, 50 brainstormed, picked with a real random number generator | Left to you, as this plan's brief says: section 1 | You run the generator; one constant in `src/data/config.js` |\n| Research, your choice | Section 19, Research row | Your report |\n| Package: one HTML file; downloadable dependencies optional | Section 16, Build | `unit`: inline scripts only. `flow`: opens from `file://` |\n| Three.js R180 | Section 16, Stack | `flow`: `THREE.REVISION` is 180 ✔ |\n| WebGPU | Section 16, Renderer | `events`: `backend` is webgpu and `render.lost` is null ✔ |\n| Stylized cel-shaded / ink filter | Section 9 | `events`: ink pixels in a read-back screenshot ✔. Art pass |\n| Rapier 0.19.3 | Sections 4 and 16 | `flow`: `RAPIER.version()` ✔ |\n| Web Audio | Section 11 | `audio`: live bus and offline renders ✔ |\n| Gamepad API | Section 3 | `input`: every verb through the stubbed pad ✔ |\n| Rest of the stack is yours | Section 16: esbuild, Playwright, Fredoka | Your report |\n| Art Direction: engine's style as base, new assets, library for generic props | Section 9, Base style and Model recipes | Art pass |\n| Animation: engine's system as base, new animations | Section 10 | `events`: the mixer's time advances and clips change ✔ |\n| Audio: all in code, no files | Section 11 | `unit`: greps. `audio`: every track, sting, effect and voice renders non-silent |\n| Voices: Animalese / Simlish-style gibberish | Section 11, Voices | `audio`: blips per line, peaks balanced |\n| Music at 10% by default | Sections 7 and 11 | `audio`: setting 10, bus gain .10 ✔ |\n| Online: AI Hub | Section 12 | `hub` ✔ |\n| Subfolder for dev files; modules bundled to one file in root | Section 16: file tree and Build | The build writes where TASK.md says; `unit` opens it |\n| External CDN allowed | Section 16: not needed, everything is inlined | `unit`: no external scripts |\n| Don't read outside your folder; shared ports and processes | Section 17, Sharing this computer | Your practice |\n| Prefer `file://` and headless tools; 127.0.0.1 on an OS-picked port; confirm the page; stop only your own PIDs | Section 17, Sharing this computer; `hub` test | `hub`: in-process server on port 0, page confirmed first ✔ |\n| Performance budget unlimited; FPS and optimisation out of scope | Section 9, Detail; section 16, Renderer | Nothing to prove: no FPS test exists on purpose |\n| Testing: scripts that play and check state, scripted whole-game playthrough | Section 17: test hooks, `flow` test | `flow` reaches the ending and beyond |\n| One command, only failures and a one-line count, rerun only after changes | Section 17, The command | Its own output ✔ |\n| Screenshots only for what a script can't check | Section 17: art pass | Your practice |\n| No subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit | Section 17, first paragraph | Your practice |\n| Effort, your choice | Section 18 gives an order; how far you take it is yours | Your report |\n| This computer: tools, browsers, WebGPU flags and secure pages | Sections 16 and 17, Browser flags | `events`: `render.lost` null ✔ |\n| Display, Firefox, WebKit, blank WebGPU screenshots | Section 17, Browser flags | `events`: WebKit boot ✔ |\n| Memory and earlyoom | Section 17, Sharing this computer | Your practice |\n| No controller, touch, listener, second player, iPhone or TV: test with the stand-ins, \"never required\" to have the real thing | Section 17, Stand-ins: these stand-ins are what TASK.md asks for | `input`, `ui`, `audio`, `hub` ✔ |\n| Sharing: browsers, `/tmp`, `playwright install`, system Python and `uv` | Section 17, Sharing this computer | Your practice |\n| Original game inspired by the source | Sections 1 and 2 | `unit` greps; your art pass and judgement |\n| Feature-complete demo, polished vertical slice, long enough to show everything | Section 1, Scope; section 5, the day table; section 18 | `flow`: every day and event, in order |\n| Capture the source's world, characters, themes and tone in mechanics, pacing and UX | Section 1's table; sections 4, 5 and 8 | `balance` for mechanics; your judgement for tone |\n| Infrastructure so the rest can be built with normal designer tools | Section 14, For later designers; `src/data/` in section 16 | `unit`: table integrity; a removed event still leaves a working programme |\n| Dev mode from the pause menu, standard tools | Section 14; Pause in section 7 | `flow`: only reachable from Pause ✔ |\n| Live sliders for the key variables, reset to defaults | Section 14 | `flow`: a slider changes the running event, reset restores it ✔ |\n| Attract mode: 10 s idle on title or menu, looping AI demo, any input exits, may cheat, shows as much as possible | Section 13 | `flow`: starts near 10 wall-clock seconds, loops, each device exits it and the press is swallowed ✔ |\n| Multiplayer: hub integration | Section 12, AI Hub session | `hub`: harness at 1, 2, 4 players, bad network, host leaving ✔ |\n| Multiplayer: the game on its own | Section 12, Group Class and On its own | `input`: four pads, four lanes ✔. `hub`: Enter and Space on its own ✔ |\n| Narrative tone like the source's | Section 8 | `unit`: lines exist, no repeats. Your judgement |\n| 10-foot, controller-first, DualSense or any standard pad | Sections 3 and 7 | `ui` at 1080p ✔. `input`: all menus by pad alone ✔ |\n| Keyboard and mouse: same game, same screens, native, expected keys | Section 3 | `input`: same effects from keys and mouse ✔. `balance` on keys |\n| Mouse aims, looks, clicks menus and world things; hover moves the focus | Section 3 | `input`: pointer weight, hover, click ✔; a parked mouse doesn't steal focus ✔ |\n| Prompts follow last input; PlayStation glyphs; Xbox option; keys and mouse buttons | Section 3 | `input`: `data-glyph` changes in the same tick ✔ |\n| No dense, web-style data screens | Section 7, The 10-foot rules | `ui`: 30 px minimum, six choices, safe area, on every screen ✔ |\n| Touch-only: no touch controls, \"Controller or keyboard required\", Settings, attract runs | Section 15 | `ui`: Pixel 7, iPhone, iPad ✔ |\n| Done: no TODOs, placeholders or stubs | Sections 18 and 19 | `unit`: greps ✔ |\n| Source Information, all 14 fields, in chat and About/Credits | Section 19: field by field, the About pages, the chat format, and the order to do it in | `ui`: About shows every value from the file ✔. `unit`: the file's shape. Your chat reply |\n| Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback, in chat and About/Credits | Section 19: every question listed, the About pages, the chat format; section 17's tally for the counts | `ui`: About shows every answer ✔. `unit`: 4, 6 and 4 answers. Your chat reply |\n| Exit-interview file: beside the HTML, exact shape, plain text, parses, `aiHub` block | Section 19: what to write and when; shape in section 22 | `unit`: parses, exact keys, every value filled |\n\n✔ here means the check was run on the integration prototype of section 20.\n\n---\n\n## 22. Planning-stage report\n\nTASK.md's reporting belongs to whoever builds the game, and only your answers count for your build (section 19 says how). Here is the same report for this planning stage, so you can see the format filled in and what was learned.\n\n**Source Information.** Date: 2026-11-04 21:19:05. Source: Wii Fit. Plan: none (this stage wrote it). Genre and Perspective: physics-balance party sports with a daily-ritual progression; third person, with framed stage cameras per event and an orbitable chase camera on the island. Tech: Three.js R180 (WebGPU, WebGL2 fallback, TSL toon and ink), Rapier 0.19.3, Web Audio, Gamepad API, esbuild 0.28.2, Playwright 1.63.0, Fredoka 5.3.0. Art Direction: cel-shaded ink over a wooden-toy resort, all models made in code. Animation: `AnimationMixer` clips on jointed rigs, physics-driven lean, Rapier ragdolls (prototyped with test clips). Audio: code-synthesised city-pop and bossa at 10% by default, effects, Animalese and Simlish-style voices (motif, voices and click prototyped). Online: AI Hub Session Protocol v1 in ghost mode for 1-4 devices, plus 1-4 on one screen. Testing: as much headless testing as wanted, with scripts that play and check state; one command printing only failures and a one-line count, rerun only after changes; screenshots only for what a script can't check; no subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit. Ran: simulations with three bot skills, and the prototype's suite. Research: the hub's protocol pages and code; source background from search summaries of Wikipedia, a Nintendo World Report review and Iwata Asks. Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux. Model: Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort max. Usage: added on import.\n\n**Testing Feedback**\n1. *How many issues did testing catch?* About 39 across the prototypes and simulations.\n2. *How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?* 13: ground contact capping lean; a parked stick scoring 100%; Plate Spinners unlosable; Plate Spinners unreachable on keys; Tug of Peace unwinnable; Tippy Raft never ending; Lean Match impossible; the fall loop after a ragdoll; silent controller play; both mouse buttons never registering together; Rapier's page-killing panic; tests \"passing\" on a lost WebGPU device; a boot snippet that wouldn't build.\n3. *What was the most helpful test you ran?* Building one event through everything from the plan's own recipes. It found the lost device, the parked mouse, the mouse-button chord, silent pad play and the fall loop, none of which any separate check had shown.\n4. *What was the least helpful test you ran?* The Firefox WebGL2 checks, which changed nothing in the game.\n\n**Engine Feedback** (Three.js and Rapier, from the prototypes)\n1. *What did you like most about the engine?* `toonOutlinePass` plus a short TSL pass gave the whole ink look, unchanged on WebGPU and WebGL2.\n2. *Did you get stuck or guess wrong?* Yes. I took a lost device for 60 fps. `hash()` given a vec2 streaked the image, toon glass turned smoky, and convex-hull stones collapsed on their own.\n3. *What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?* The balance core: a torque-limited controller with gravity feed-forward on a ball joint.\n4. *What was the hardest part about using the engine?* Knowing what was really happening. A lost WebGPU device gives no error and a loop that seems to run. A Rapier crash surfaces half a second after its cause, only once something moves.\n5. *What did you change in the engine, and why?* None.\n6. *If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?* Make Rapier's panic on a stale handle or a bad removal a catchable error, instead of killing all physics for the page.\n\n**Prompt Feedback**\n1. *What did you like most about this prompt?* Open creative choices combined with exact, checkable facts about the machine and the hub.\n2. *What was confusing?* What \"the game on its own\" means for multiplayer; \"native style\" and \"asset library\" for an engine with neither; and which parts of TASK.md apply at the plan stage.\n3. *What was the hardest thing to do?* Making a balance-board game that a steady thumb can't trivially beat, while staying original.\n4. *What would you change in this prompt?* Fix the WebGPU flags, which lose the device within a second. Define standalone multiplayer. State how capable builders are.\n\n**The exit-interview file's shape,** filled in for this stage. It parses, and its keys match TASK.md's exactly. Replace every value with your own. `name` and `tagline` are \"unknown\" here only because each builder picks its own title.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"sourceInformation\": {\n    \"date\": \"2026-11-04 21:19:05\",\n    \"source\": \"Wii Fit\",\n    \"plan\": \"none\",\n    \"genre\": \"Physics-balance party sports with a daily-ritual progression\",\n    \"perspective\": \"Third person: framed stage cameras per event, orbitable chase camera on the island\",\n    \"tech\": \"Three.js R180 (WebGPU, WebGL2 fallback, TSL toon and ink), Rapier 0.19.3, Web Audio, Gamepad API, esbuild 0.28.2, Playwright 1.63.0, Fredoka 5.3.0\",\n    \"artDirection\": \"Cel-shaded ink over a wooden-toy resort, all models made in code\",\n    \"animation\": \"AnimationMixer clips on jointed rigs, physics-driven lean, Rapier ragdolls\",\n    \"audio\": \"Code-synthesised city-pop and bossa at 10% by default, effects, Animalese and Simlish-style voices\",\n    \"online\": \"AI Hub Session Protocol v1 in ghost mode for 1-4 devices, plus 1-4 on one screen\",\n    \"testing\": \"Headless scripts that play and check state, one command printing only failures and a count, screenshots only where needed, no subagents or workflows to review\",\n    \"research\": \"Hub protocol pages and code; source background from search summaries\",\n    \"environment\": \"Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\",\n    \"model\": \"Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort max\",\n    \"usage\": \"added on import\"\n  },\n  \"testingFeedback\": [\n    \"About 39 across the prototypes and simulations.\",\n    \"13.\",\n    \"Building one event through everything from the plan's own recipes.\",\n    \"The Firefox WebGL2 checks.\"\n  ],\n  \"engineFeedback\": [\n    \"toonOutlinePass plus a short TSL pass gave the whole ink look on both backends.\",\n    \"Yes. I took a lost device for 60 fps.\",\n    \"The balance core.\",\n    \"Knowing what was really happening: a lost device gives no error.\",\n    \"None.\",\n    \"Make Rapier's panic a catchable error.\"\n  ],\n  \"promptFeedback\": [\n    \"Open creative choices combined with exact, checkable facts.\",\n    \"What the game on its own means for multiplayer.\",\n    \"Making a balance-board game a steady thumb can't trivially beat.\",\n    \"Fix the WebGPU flags, which lose the device within a second.\"\n  ],\n  \"aiHub\": {\n    \"name\": \"unknown\",\n    \"tagline\": \"unknown\",\n    \"description\": \"A wooden-toy wellness retreat on a crooked island, where you hold poses, stack stones and catch fruit with real balance physics, and straighten the whole island one class at a time.\",\n    \"type\": \"Game\",\n    \"genre\": \"Sports\",\n    \"source\": \"Wii Fit\",\n    \"series\": \"\",\n    \"players\": \"1–4\",\n    \"controls\": \"Lean: stick, WASD or mouse. Feet: L2 R2, Q E or clicks. Flail: Square or F\",\n    \"tags\": [\n      \"3d\",\n      \"comedy\",\n      \"third-person\",\n      \"versus\",\n      \"arcade\"\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```\n","numLines":263,"startLine":851,"totalLines":1113}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01VAXWhVU1QmznoqTc7zY8Ya","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
